4           -0.020626        0.874938           0.017666 moderate_risk      monitor\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1320x396 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Interpreting Section 5\n\nThree points a reader should hold onto:\n\n1. **The verdict rule reads development evidence only.** `PSI_dev_vs_oot` is\n   computed and exported because RQ7 asks about OOT generalization risk, but\n   `assign_verdict` never reads it. That separation is the difference between\n   *reporting* on the holdout and *deciding* with it.\n2. **A tiny KS p-value is not a finding.** With ~120,000 rows per block, KS\n   detects differences far below anything that would change a model's behaviour.\n   The statistic (a maximum CDF gap) is the effect size; the adjusted p-value\n   only says the gap is not sampling noise.\n3. **Lift stability is withheld, not defaulted.** A feature whose top decile\n   contains fewer than the configured minimum positives returns\n   `insufficient_evidence`, which propagates into the ledger as `null` rather\n   than as a flattering number.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 6. Missingness Stability and Confounding\n\nColumns blank on exactly the same rows are collapsed into one **missingness\npattern** before testing. Without this, a single underlying event — most often\n\"the identity table had no matching row\" — is counted once per column, inflating\nboth the discovery count and the multiplicity correction.\n\nEach pattern then gets: a missing rate by block, fraud rates for the missing and\nobserved groups by block with their positive counts, a ProductCD-stratified\nrepeat of the same contrast, a chi-square test with BH correction, a\ndirection-consistency requirement across development blocks, and an explicit\ncheck for sign reversal inside strata.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\"\"\"Section 6 - missingness stability, ProductCD stratification and confounding.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef analyze_missingness_by_block(frame: pd.DataFrame, features: Sequence[str],\n                                 target: str = TARGET_COLUMN,\n                                 block_column: str = \"chrono_block\",\n                                 min_group_n: int = CONFIG[\"MIN_GROUP_N\"]) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"Missing rate and fraud rate for the missing vs observed group, per block.\n\n    Every returned rate carries its own denominator (``n_missing`` /\n    ``n_observed``) so no percentage in the output is unattributable. Groups\n    smaller than ``min_group_n`` are reported with ``reliable = False`` rather\n    than dropped, because their absence would itself be informative.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not features:\n        raise ValueError(\"analyze_missingness_by_block requires at least one feature.\")\n    rows = []\n    for feature in features:\n        indicator = frame[feature].isna()\n        for block, idx in frame.groupby(block_column).groups.items():\n            sub_missing = indicator.loc[idx]\n            y = frame.loc[idx, target]\n            n_missing, n_observed = int(sub_missing.sum()), int((~sub_missing).sum())\n            rows.append({\n                \"feature\": feature, \"block\": int(block),\n                \"n\": len(idx), \"n_missing\": n_missing, \"n_observed\": n_observed,\n                \"missing_rate\": n_missing / len(idx) if len(idx) else np.nan,\n                \"positives_missing\": int(y[sub_missing].sum()),\n                \"positives_observed\": int(y[~sub_missing].sum()),\n                \"fraud_rate_missing\": float(y[sub_missing].mean()) if n_missing else np.nan,\n                \"fraud_rate_observed\": float(y[~sub_missing].mean()) if n_observed else np.nan,\n                \"reliable\": min(n_missing, n_observed) >= min_group_n,\n            })\n    out = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n    out[\"delta_pp\"] = 100 * (out[\"fraud_rate_missing\"] - out[\"fraud_rate_observed\"])\n    return out\n\n\ndef analyze_missingness_by_product(frame: pd.DataFrame, features: Sequence[str],\n                                   product_column: str = \"ProductCD\",\n                                   target: str = TARGET_COLUMN,\n                                   min_group_n: int = CONFIG[\"MIN_GROUP_N\"]) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"Same contrast as ``analyze_missingness_by_block`` but stratified by product.\n\n    The aggregate \"missing implies more/less fraud\" relationship can invert\n    inside a stratum when ProductCD drives both the missingness and the fraud\n    rate. This function produces the per-stratum evidence needed to check that,\n    and the caller compares the signs.\n    \"\"\"\n    if product_column not in frame.columns:\n        raise KeyError(f\"'{product_column}' is not present; stratified analysis \"\n                       \"cannot run and must be recorded as not computed.\")\n    rows = []\n    for feature in features:\n        indicator = frame[feature].isna()\n        for product, idx in frame.groupby(product_column, observed=True).groups.items():\n            sub_missing = indicator.loc[idx]\n            y = frame.loc[idx, target]\n            n_missing, n_observed = int(sub_missing.sum()), int((~sub_missing).sum())\n            rows.append({\n                \"feature\": feature, \"product\": product, \"n\": len(idx),\n                \"n_missing\": n_missing, \"n_observed\": n_observed,\n                \"positives_missing\": int(y[sub_missing].sum()),\n                \"positives_observed\": int(y[~sub_missing].sum()),\n                \"fraud_rate_missing\": float(y[sub_missing].mean()) if n_missing else np.nan,\n                \"fraud_rate_observed\": float(y[~sub_missing].mean()) if n_observed else np.nan,\n                \"reliable\": min(n_missing, n_observed) >= min_group_n,\n            })\n    out = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n    out[\"delta_pp\"] = 100 * (out[\"fraud_rate_missing\"] - out[\"fraud_rate_observed\"])\n    return out\n\n\ndef group_by_missingness_pattern(frame: pd.DataFrame, features: Sequence[str]) -> Dict[str, List[str]]:\n    \"\"\"Collapse columns that are blank on exactly the same rows into one pattern.\n\n    Testing each column separately would count one underlying signal (for\n    example \"the identity table had no matching row\") once per column and\n    inflate both the discovery count and the multiplicity correction. Hashing\n    the boolean missing-mask is O(n) per column instead of O(n) per pair.\n    Representative = the first column in deterministic sorted order.\n    \"\"\"\n    buckets: Dict[Any, List[str]] = {}\n    for column in features:\n        key = hashlib.sha1(frame[column].isna().to_numpy().tobytes()).hexdigest()\n        buckets.setdefault(key, []).append(column)\n    return {sorted(members)[0]: sorted(members) for members in buckets.values()}\n\n\n# --- candidate features: those with genuinely partial missingness in dev -----\n_missing_rate_dev = dev_df[FEATURE_COLUMNS].isna().mean()\nMISSINGNESS_CANDIDATES = sorted(_missing_rate_dev[(_missing_rate_dev > CONFIG[\"PARTIAL_MISSING_MIN_RATE\"])\n                                                  & (_missing_rate_dev < CONFIG[\"PARTIAL_MISSING_MAX_RATE\"])].index)\nPATTERN_GROUPS = group_by_missingness_pattern(dev_df, MISSINGNESS_CANDIDATES)\nPATTERN_LEADS = sorted(PATTERN_GROUPS)\nLOG.info(\"%d partially-missing columns collapse to %d distinct missingness patterns.\",\n         len(MISSINGNESS_CANDIDATES), len(PATTERN_LEADS))\n\nAUDITOR.guard(\"missingness_pattern_tests\", dev_df[[ID_COLUMN]])\nMISSING_BY_BLOCK = analyze_missingness_by_block(dev_df, PATTERN_LEADS)\nMISSING_BY_PRODUCT = analyze_missingness_by_product(dev_df, PATTERN_LEADS)\n\n# --- one chi-square test per PATTERN, on development rows, BH-corrected -----\n_rows = []\n_y_dev = dev_df[TARGET_COLUMN]\nfor lead in PATTERN_LEADS:\n    blank = dev_df[lead].isna()\n    n_blank, n_filled = int(blank.sum()), int((~blank).sum())\n    if min(n_blank, n_filled) < CONFIG[\"MIN_GROUP_N\"]:\n        _rows.append({\"pattern_lead\": lead, \"n_columns\": len(PATTERN_GROUPS[lead]),\n                      \"n_blank\": n_blank, \"n_filled\": n_filled, \"delta_pp\": np.nan,\n                      \"phi\": np.nan, \"pvalue\": np.nan, \"tested\": False,\n                      \"reason\": f\"a group has fewer than {CONFIG['MIN_GROUP_N']} rows\"})\n        continue\n    k_blank, k_filled = int(_y_dev[blank].sum()), int(_y_dev[~blank].sum())\n    table = np.array([[k_blank, n_blank - k_blank], [k_filled, n_filled - k_filled]])\n    chi2, pvalue, _, _ = stats.chi2_contingency(table)\n    delta_pp = 100 * (k_blank / n_blank - k_filled / n_filled)\n    # Per-block sign consistency within development.\n    signs = []\n    for block in DEV_BLOCKS:\n        m = dev_df[\"chrono_block\"] == block\n        if (blank & m).sum() >= CONFIG[\"MIN_BLOCK_GROUP_N\"] and ((~blank) & m).sum() >= CONFIG[\"MIN_BLOCK_GROUP_N\"]:\n            signs.append(np.sign(_y_dev[blank & m].mean() - _y_dev[(~blank) & m].mean()))\n    consistency = float(np.mean(np.array(signs) == np.sign(delta_pp))) if signs else np.nan\n    _rows.append({\"pattern_lead\": lead, \"n_columns\": len(PATTERN_GROUPS[lead]),\n                  \"n_blank\": n_blank, \"n_filled\": n_filled,\n                  \"delta_pp\": round(delta_pp, 3),\n                  \"phi\": round(float(np.sqrt(chi2 / len(_y_dev))), 4),\n                  \"pvalue\": float(pvalue), \"direction_consistency\": consistency,\n                  \"tested\": True, \"reason\": \"\"})\n\nMISSINGNESS_TESTS = pd.DataFrame(_rows)\n_adj = adjust_multiple_tests(MISSINGNESS_TESTS[\"pvalue\"].to_numpy())\nMISSINGNESS_TESTS[\"adjusted_pvalue\"] = _adj[\"adjusted_pvalue\"].to_numpy()\nMISSINGNESS_TESTS[\"reject_bh\"] = _adj[\"reject\"].to_numpy()\nMISSINGNESS_TESTS[\"keep_indicator\"] = (\n    MISSINGNESS_TESTS[\"reject_bh\"]\n    & (MISSINGNESS_TESTS[\"delta_pp\"].abs() >= CONFIG[\"MIN_ABS_DELTA_PP\"])\n    & (MISSINGNESS_TESTS[\"direction_consistency\"] >= 0.75)\n)\n\n# --- Simpson / confounding check against ProductCD --------------------------\n_conf_rows = []\nfor lead in PATTERN_LEADS:\n    aggregate = MISSINGNESS_TESTS.loc[MISSINGNESS_TESTS.pattern_lead == lead, \"delta_pp\"]\n    if aggregate.empty or not np.isfinite(aggregate.iloc[0]):\n        continue\n    agg_sign = np.sign(aggregate.iloc[0])\n    strata = MISSING_BY_PRODUCT[(MISSING_BY_PRODUCT.feature == lead)\n                                & MISSING_BY_PRODUCT.reliable\n                                & MISSING_BY_PRODUCT.delta_pp.notna()]\n    if strata.empty:\n        _conf_rows.append({\"pattern_lead\": lead, \"n_reliable_strata\": 0,\n                           \"share_agreeing\": np.nan, \"flag\": \"no_reliable_strata\"})\n        continue\n    agree = float((np.sign(strata.delta_pp) == agg_sign).mean())\n    _conf_rows.append({\n        \"pattern_lead\": lead, \"n_reliable_strata\": int(len(strata)),\n        \"share_agreeing\": round(agree, 3),\n        \"flag\": (\"sign_reverses_in_some_strata\" if agree < 1.0 else \"consistent\"),\n    })\nCONFOUNDING = pd.DataFrame(_conf_rows)\n\n_reversals = CONFOUNDING[CONFOUNDING.flag == \"sign_reverses_in_some_strata\"]\nprint(f\"Patterns tested: {int(MISSINGNESS_TESTS.tested.sum())} of {len(MISSINGNESS_TESTS)}; \"\n      f\"surviving BH + effect size + direction consistency: \"\n      f\"{int(MISSINGNESS_TESTS.keep_indicator.sum())}.\")\nprint(f\"Patterns whose aggregate direction reverses inside at least one reliable \"\n      f\"ProductCD stratum: {len(_reversals)}. A reversal is a Simpson-type warning: \"\n      \"the aggregate association is at least partly a product-mix effect. This is an \"\n      \"association, not evidence that blankness causes fraud.\")\nprint(\"\\nTop patterns by |effect size|:\")\nprint(MISSINGNESS_TESTS.reindex(MISSINGNESS_TESTS.delta_pp.abs().sort_values(ascending=False).index)\n      .head(12)[[\"pattern_lead\", \"n_columns\", \"n_blank\", \"n_filled\", \"delta_pp\",\n                 \"phi\", \"adjusted_pvalue\", \"direction_consistency\", \"keep_indicator\"]]\n      .to_string(index=False))\n\n_top = (MISSINGNESS_TESTS.reindex(MISSINGNESS_TESTS.delta_pp.abs()\n                                  .sort_values(ascending=False).index)\n        .head(6)[\"pattern_lead\"].tolist())\nif _top:\n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13, 4))\n    for lead in _top:\n        series = MISSING_BY_BLOCK[MISSING_BY_BLOCK.feature == lead]\n        axes[0].plot(series.block, 100 * series.missing_rate, marker=\"o\", label=lead)\n        axes[1].plot(series.block, series.delta_pp, marker=\"o\", label=lead)\n    axes[0].set_xlabel(\"development block\")\n    axes[0].set_ylabel(\"missing rate (%)\")\n    axes[0].set_title(\"Missingness rate by block (development only)\")\n    axes[1].axhline(0, color=\"grey\", lw=1)\n    axes[1].set_xlabel(\"development block\")\n    axes[1].set_ylabel(\"fraud rate: missing minus observed (pp)\")\n    axes[1].set_title(\"Is the missingness signal stable over time?\")\n    for ax in axes:\n        ax.set_xticks(DEV_BLOCKS)\n        ax.legend(fontsize=7, ncol=2)\n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.show()\n\n    _strata = MISSING_BY_PRODUCT[MISSING_BY_PRODUCT.feature.isin(_top)\n                                 & MISSING_BY_PRODUCT.reliable]\n    if not _strata.empty:\n        fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 3.6))\n        for lead, part in _strata.groupby(\"feature\"):\n            ax.scatter(part[\"product\"].astype(str), part.delta_pp, label=lead, s=45)\n        ax.axhline(0, color=\"grey\", lw=1)\n        ax.set_xlabel(\"ProductCD stratum\")\n        ax.set_ylabel(\"fraud rate: missing minus observed (pp)\")\n        ax.set_title(\"Missingness effect within ProductCD strata \"\n                     \"(points below zero reverse the usual aggregate direction)\")\n        ax.legend(fontsize=7, ncol=3)\n        plt.tight_layout()\n        plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-24T03:22:25.187159Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-24T03:22:25.188457Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-24T03:22:42.996658Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-24T03:22:25.188391Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-24T03:22:42.995656Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"03:22:27 | INFO    | 339 partially-missing columns collapse to 65 distinct missingness patterns.\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stdout","text":"Patterns tested: 65 of 65; surviving BH + effect size + direction consistency: 58.\nPatterns whose aggregate direction reverses inside at least one reliable ProductCD stratum: 32. A reversal is a Simpson-type warning: the aggregate association is at least partly a product-mix effect. This is an association, not evidence that blankness causes fraud.\n\nTop patterns by |effect size|:\n pattern_lead  n_columns  n_blank  n_filled  delta_pp    phi  adjusted_pvalue  direction_consistency  keep_indicator\n           D7          1   442488     29944   -12.230 0.1618              0.0                    1.0            True\n        addr1          2    53761    418671     8.898 0.1534              0.0                    1.0            True\n          D14          1   422596     49836    -8.885 0.1482              0.0                    1.0            True\n          D12          1   419729     52703    -8.844 0.1512              0.0                    1.0            True\n          D13          1   423459     48973    -8.193 0.1356              0.0                    1.0            True\n        id_03          2   418342     54090    -7.818 0.1352              0.0                    1.0            True\n           D6          1   413949     58483    -7.759 0.1388              0.0                    1.0            True\n           D8          4   410822     61610    -7.620 0.1393              0.0                    1.0            True\n        dist2          1   440332     32100    -6.401 0.0875              0.0                    1.0            True\n        id_13          1   367262    105170    -5.821 0.1315              0.0                    1.0            True\nR_emaildomain          1   358812    113620    -5.818 0.1350              0.0                    1.0            True\n        id_05          2   357945    114487    -5.580 0.1299              0.0                    1.0            True\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1430x440 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1100x396 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":10},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Interpreting Section 6\n\nA **sign reversal inside a ProductCD stratum is a Simpson-type warning**: the\naggregate contrast is at least partly a product-mix effect, because ProductCD\ndrives both the availability of the field and the base fraud rate. The\nstratified panel is what separates the two; the aggregate number alone cannot.\n\nA negative gap — blank implies *less* fraud — is usually the join, not the\ncustomer. Before any such indicator is carried into a model it must be checked\nagainst the identity-table coverage, because \"no identity record existed\" is a\ndata-collection fact, not a behavioural one.\n\nNothing in this section supports a causal reading. Missingness is associated with\nthe outcome; that is the entire claim.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 7. V-Series Redundancy and Stability\n\nDevelopment data only, in eight deterministic steps: per-block Spearman\ncorrelation → distance `1 − |ρ|` → average-linkage hierarchical clustering →\ncross-block co-membership → retain only clusters stable across development blocks\n→ pick one representative per stable cluster by mutual information with the\ntarget on development rows → deterministic tie-breaking (MI, then completeness,\nthen name) → record why each representative won.\n\n**Approximation, labelled as such:** each block's correlation matrix is estimated\non an evenly-spaced sample of that block when it exceeds the configured row\nbudget. Evenly spaced rather than random keeps the within-block chronology and\nkeeps the result reproducible without a seed. The sampling note is printed per\nblock and stored with the run.\n\nNo reducer is fitted. Deciding what *could* be reduced is EDA; performing the\nreduction is modelling, and a reducer fitted here would have to be refitted\ninside each training fold anyway to stay leakage-safe.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\"\"\"Section 7 - V-series redundancy, cross-block stability and representatives.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef evenly_spaced_sample(frame: pd.DataFrame, max_rows: int) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"Return at most ``max_rows`` deterministic, evenly spaced rows.\"\"\"\n    if max_rows < 1:\n        raise ValueError(f\"max_rows must be positive, got {max_rows}.\")\n    if len(frame) <= max_rows:\n        return frame\n    positions = np.linspace(0, len(frame) - 1, num=max_rows, dtype=np.int64)\n    return frame.iloc[positions]\n\n\ndef cluster_v_features_by_block(frame: pd.DataFrame, features: Sequence[str],\n                                block: int, block_column: str = \"chrono_block\",\n                                distance_threshold: float = CONFIG[\"CLUSTER_DISTANCE_THRESHOLD\"],\n                                sample_rows: int = CONFIG[\"CORR_SAMPLE_ROWS\"],\n                                seed: int = RANDOM_SEED) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Cluster features within ONE block using distance = 1 - |Spearman|.\n\n    An evenly-spaced (not random) sample is drawn when the block exceeds\n    ``sample_rows``, which preserves the chronology inside the block instead of\n    over-weighting any part of it, and is deterministic. Average linkage on the\n    condensed distance matrix keeps memory at O(k^2) for k features and avoids\n    any dense row-by-row adjacency structure.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict with cluster_map (feature -> cluster id), n_features, n_rows_used,\n        block, and the approximation note describing any sampling applied.\n    \"\"\"\n    block_frame = frame.loc[frame[block_column] == block, list(features)]\n    n_rows = len(block_frame)\n    note = \"full block\"\n    if n_rows > sample_rows:\n        block_frame = evenly_spaced_sample(block_frame, sample_rows)\n        note = (f\"evenly spaced deterministic sample of the block \"\n                f\"({len(block_frame):,} of {n_rows:,} rows); APPROXIMATION\")\n    usable = [c for c in features if block_frame[c].notna().sum() >= CONFIG[\"MIN_NON_MISSING\"]\n              and block_frame[c].nunique(dropna=True) > 1]\n    if len(usable) < 2:\n        return {\"cluster_map\": {}, \"n_features\": len(usable), \"n_rows_used\": len(block_frame),\n                \"block\": int(block), \"note\": note + \"; too few usable features to cluster\"}\n    corr = block_frame[usable].corr(method=\"spearman\", min_periods=CONFIG[\"MIN_NON_MISSING\"])\n    distance = (1 - corr.abs()).to_numpy()\n    distance = np.nan_to_num(distance, nan=1.0)\n    np.fill_diagonal(distance, 0.0)\n    distance = np.clip((distance + distance.T) / 2, 0, None)\n    linkage_matrix = linkage(squareform(distance, checks=False), method=\"average\")\n    labels = fcluster(linkage_matrix, t=distance_threshold, criterion=\"distance\")\n    return {\"cluster_map\": {f: int(l) for f, l in zip(usable, labels)},\n            \"n_features\": len(usable), \"n_rows_used\": int(len(block_frame)),\n            \"block\": int(block), \"note\": note}\n\n\ndef assess_cluster_stability(cluster_maps: Mapping[int, Mapping[str, int]],\n                             features: Sequence[str]) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"Measure how often two features co-cluster across development blocks.\n\n    For every feature pair assessable in at least two blocks, the co-membership\n    rate is the share of those blocks in which the pair shares a cluster. A\n    feature's ``cluster_stability`` is the mean co-membership rate over the\n    partners it shares its reference-block cluster with; a singleton scores 1.0\n    only if it is a singleton everywhere, and NaN when it was never assessable.\n    \"\"\"\n    blocks = sorted(cluster_maps)\n    if len(blocks) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Cluster stability needs at least two blocks.\")\n    reference = cluster_maps[blocks[0]]\n    rows = []\n    for feature in features:\n        if feature not in reference:\n            rows.append({\"feature\": feature, \"cluster_id\": None,\n                         \"cluster_stability\": np.nan, \"n_blocks_assessed\": 0,\n                         \"partners\": \"\"})\n            continue\n        partners = [f for f, c in reference.items()\n                    if c == reference[feature] and f != feature]\n        assessed = [b for b in blocks if feature in cluster_maps[b]]\n        if not partners:\n            singleton_rate = float(np.mean([\n                sum(1 for f, c in cluster_maps[b].items()\n                    if c == cluster_maps[b][feature] and f != feature) == 0\n                for b in assessed])) if assessed else np.nan\n            rows.append({\"feature\": feature, \"cluster_id\": int(reference[feature]),\n                         \"cluster_stability\": singleton_rate,\n                         \"n_blocks_assessed\": len(assessed), \"partners\": \"\"})\n            continue\n        rates = []\n        for partner in partners:\n            shared = [b for b in assessed if partner in cluster_maps[b]]\n            if shared:\n                rates.append(np.mean([cluster_maps[b][feature] == cluster_maps[b][partner]\n                                      for b in shared]))\n        rows.append({\"feature\": feature, \"cluster_id\": int(reference[feature]),\n                     \"cluster_stability\": float(np.mean(rates)) if rates else np.nan,\n                     \"n_blocks_assessed\": len(assessed),\n                     \"partners\": \", \".join(sorted(partners)[:8])\n                                 + (\" ...\" if len(partners) > 8 else \"\")})\n    return pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\n\ndef select_cluster_representatives(frame: pd.DataFrame, stability: pd.DataFrame,\n                                   target: str = TARGET_COLUMN,\n                                   stability_threshold: float = CONFIG[\"CLUSTER_STABILITY_THRESHOLD\"],\n                                   sample_rows: int = CONFIG[\"CORR_SAMPLE_ROWS\"],\n                                   seed: int = RANDOM_SEED) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"Pick one representative per sufficiently stable cluster.\n\n    Ranking key, applied in this exact order so ties are broken deterministically\n    and never by dict or file ordering:\n        1. higher mutual information with the target (DEVELOPMENT rows only),\n        2. lower development missing rate,\n        3. lexicographically smaller feature name.\n\n    ``frame`` must be development-only; the caller passes it through the leakage\n    auditor first, and this function never receives an OOT target column.\n    \"\"\"\n    if target not in frame.columns:\n        raise KeyError(f\"'{target}' is required to score representatives.\")\n    stable = stability[stability.cluster_id.notna()\n                       & (stability.cluster_stability >= stability_threshold)]\n    rows = []\n    for cluster_id, members in stable.groupby(\"cluster_id\"):\n        columns = sorted(members.feature)\n        subset = frame[columns + [target]]\n        if len(subset) > sample_rows:\n            subset = evenly_spaced_sample(subset, sample_rows)\n        filled = subset[columns].astype(float)\n        filled = filled.replace([np.inf, -np.inf], np.nan)\n        # Median imputation for the MI estimate only; medians come from the same\n        # development sample and are never applied to OOT or to the modelling data.\n        filled = filled.fillna(filled.median(numeric_only=True)).fillna(0.0)\n        try:\n            mi = mutual_info_classif(filled.to_numpy(), subset[target].to_numpy(),\n                                     discrete_features=False, random_state=seed)\n        except ValueError as exc:\n            LOG.warning(\"Mutual information failed for cluster %s (%s). Falling back \"\n                        \"to the completeness + name ordering and recording the \"\n                        \"fallback in the reason.\", cluster_id, exc)\n            mi = np.full(len(columns), np.nan)\n        missing_rate = frame[columns].isna().mean()\n        ranked = sorted(\n            range(len(columns)),\n            key=lambda i: (-(mi[i] if np.isfinite(mi[i]) else -1.0),\n                           float(missing_rate[columns[i]]), columns[i]),\n        )\n        best = ranked[0]\n        for i, column in enumerate(columns):\n            rows.append({\n                \"cluster_id\": int(cluster_id), \"feature\": column,\n                \"cluster_size\": len(columns),\n                \"mutual_information_dev\": float(mi[i]) if np.isfinite(mi[i]) else None,\n                \"development_missing_rate\": float(missing_rate[column]),\n                \"is_representative\": i == best,\n                \"selection_reason\": (\n                    \"highest development mutual information; ties broken by lower \"\n                    \"missing rate then lexicographic name\"\n                    if i == best else\n                    f\"redundant with {columns[best]} (same stable cluster)\"),\n            })\n    return pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\n\nif V_SERIES:\n    _v_missing_dev = dev_df[V_SERIES].isna().mean()\n    V_USABLE = sorted([c for c in V_SERIES\n                       if _v_missing_dev[c] * 100 < CONFIG[\"V_MAX_MISSING_PCT\"]],\n                      key=lambda c: int(c[1:]))\n    V_TOO_SPARSE = [c for c in V_SERIES if c not in V_USABLE]\n    print(f\"Workload estimate: {len(V_USABLE)} V columns x {len(DEV_BLOCKS)} development \"\n          f\"blocks; each block correlates a {len(V_USABLE)}x{len(V_USABLE)} Spearman \"\n          f\"matrix on at most {CONFIG['CORR_SAMPLE_ROWS']:,} rows \"\n          f\"(~{len(V_USABLE) ** 2 * 8 / 1e6:.0f} MB per matrix, one at a time).\")\n\n    CLUSTER_MAPS: Dict[int, Dict[str, int]] = {}\n    CLUSTER_NOTES: Dict[int, str] = {}\n    for block in DEV_BLOCKS:\n        AUDITOR.guard(f\"v_cluster_block{block}\", df.loc[df.chrono_block == block, [ID_COLUMN]])\n        result = cluster_v_features_by_block(dev_df, V_USABLE, block=block)\n        CLUSTER_MAPS[block] = result[\"cluster_map\"]\n        CLUSTER_NOTES[block] = result[\"note\"]\n        AUDITOR.register_fit(f\"v_clustering_block{block}\", \"clustering\",\n                             df.loc[df.chrono_block == block, ID_COLUMN].to_numpy(), [block])\n        LOG.info(\"Block %d: %d features clustered into %d clusters (%s).\", block,\n                 result[\"n_features\"], len(set(result[\"cluster_map\"].values())), result[\"note\"])\n\n    CLUSTER_STABILITY = assess_cluster_stability(CLUSTER_MAPS, V_USABLE)\n    AUDITOR.guard(\"v_representative_selection\", dev_df[[ID_COLUMN]])\n    V_REPRESENTATIVES = select_cluster_representatives(dev_df, CLUSTER_STABILITY)\n    AUDITOR.register_fit(\"v_representative_selection\", \"feature_selection\",\n                         dev_df[ID_COLUMN].to_numpy(), DEV_BLOCKS)\n\n    V_SUMMARY = (CLUSTER_STABILITY\n                 .merge(V_REPRESENTATIVES[[\"feature\", \"cluster_size\",\n                                           \"mutual_information_dev\", \"is_representative\",\n                                           \"selection_reason\"]],\n                        on=\"feature\", how=\"left\")\n                 .merge(feature_risk[[\"feature\", \"PSI_max_development_only\", \"verdict\"]]\n                        .rename(columns={\"verdict\": \"temporal_verdict\"}),\n                        on=\"feature\", how=\"left\"))\n    V_SUMMARY[\"development_missing_rate\"] = V_SUMMARY[\"feature\"].map(_v_missing_dev)\n    V_SUMMARY[\"stable_cluster\"] = V_SUMMARY[\"cluster_stability\"] >= CONFIG[\"CLUSTER_STABILITY_THRESHOLD\"]\n    V_SUMMARY[\"final_action\"] = np.where(\n        ~V_SUMMARY[\"stable_cluster\"].fillna(False), \"monitor\",\n        np.where(V_SUMMARY[\"is_representative\"].fillna(False), \"keep\", \"tag_not_drop\"))\n\n    _multi = V_SUMMARY[V_SUMMARY.cluster_size.fillna(1) > 1]\n    print(f\"\\nUsable V columns: {len(V_USABLE)} (excluded as too sparse: {len(V_TOO_SPARSE)}).\")\n    print(f\"Clusters judged stable across all {len(DEV_BLOCKS)} development blocks \"\n          f\"(co-membership >= {CONFIG['CLUSTER_STABILITY_THRESHOLD']}): \"\n          f\"{int(V_SUMMARY.loc[V_SUMMARY.stable_cluster.fillna(False), 'cluster_id'].nunique())}.\")\n    print(f\"Columns inside a stable multi-member cluster: {len(_multi)}; \"\n          f\"representatives kept: {int(V_SUMMARY.is_representative.fillna(False).sum())}.\")\n    print(\"\\nLargest stable clusters:\")\n    print(_multi.sort_values([\"cluster_size\", \"cluster_id\", \"feature\"], ascending=[False, True, True])\n          .head(15)[[\"cluster_id\", \"feature\", \"cluster_size\", \"cluster_stability\",\n                     \"mutual_information_dev\", \"development_missing_rate\",\n                     \"is_representative\", \"final_action\"]].to_string(index=False))\n    print(\"\\nNo dimensionality reduction is FITTED here. Redundancy is a diagnosis; \"\n          \"any reducer must be refitted inside each training fold to stay leakage-safe.\")\n\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 3.4))\n    ax.hist(CLUSTER_STABILITY.cluster_stability.dropna(), bins=20)\n    ax.axvline(CONFIG[\"CLUSTER_STABILITY_THRESHOLD\"], color=\"crimson\", ls=\"--\",\n               label=f\"stability threshold {CONFIG['CLUSTER_STABILITY_THRESHOLD']}\")\n    ax.set_xlabel(\"cross-block co-membership rate\")\n    ax.set_ylabel(\"V features\")\n    ax.set_title(\"How reproducible is each V-series redundancy grouping across \"\n                 \"development blocks?\")\n    ax.legend(fontsize=8)\n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.show()\nelse:\n    V_USABLE, V_TOO_SPARSE, CLUSTER_MAPS = [], [], {}\n    CLUSTER_STABILITY = pd.DataFrame(columns=[\"feature\", \"cluster_id\", \"cluster_stability\"])\n    V_REPRESENTATIVES = pd.DataFrame(columns=[\"feature\", \"is_representative\"])\n    V_SUMMARY = pd.DataFrame(columns=[\"feature\", \"cluster_id\", \"final_action\"])\n    LOG.warning(\"No V-series columns: Section 7 is recorded as NOT COMPUTED.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-24T03:31:53.242717Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-24T03:31:53.246959Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-24T03:40:29.869148Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-24T03:31:53.246798Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-24T03:40:29.867783Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Workload estimate: 339 V columns x 4 development blocks; each block correlates a 339x339 Spearman matrix on at most 60,000 rows (~1 MB per matrix, one at a time).\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stderr","text":"03:34:01 | INFO    | Block 1: 338 features clustered into 143 clusters (evenly spaced deterministic sample of the block (60,000 of 118,108 rows); APPROXIMATION).\n03:35:41 | INFO    | Block 2: 337 features clustered into 159 clusters (evenly spaced deterministic sample of the block (60,000 of 118,108 rows); APPROXIMATION).\n03:37:17 | INFO    | Block 3: 336 features clustered into 153 clusters (evenly spaced deterministic sample of the block (60,000 of 118,108 rows); APPROXIMATION).\n03:39:10 | INFO    | Block 4: 339 features clustered into 156 clusters (evenly spaced deterministic sample of the block (60,000 of 118,108 rows); APPROXIMATION).\n/tmp/ipykernel_58/4029322801.py:205: FutureWarning: Downcasting object dtype arrays on .fillna, .ffill, .bfill is deprecated and will change in a future version. Call result.infer_objects(copy=False) instead. To opt-in to the future behavior, set `pd.set_option('future.no_silent_downcasting', True)`\n  np.where(V_SUMMARY[\"is_representative\"].fillna(False), \"keep\", \"tag_not_drop\"))\n/tmp/ipykernel_58/4029322801.py:213: FutureWarning: Downcasting object dtype arrays on .fillna, .ffill, .bfill is deprecated and will change in a future version. Call result.infer_objects(copy=False) instead. To opt-in to the future behavior, set `pd.set_option('future.no_silent_downcasting', True)`\n  f\"representatives kept: {int(V_SUMMARY.is_representative.fillna(False).sum())}.\")\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stdout","text":"\nUsable V columns: 339 (excluded as too sparse: 0).\nClusters judged stable across all 4 development blocks (co-membership >= 0.75): 119.\nColumns inside a stable multi-member cluster: 172; representatives kept: 119.\n\nLargest stable clusters:\n cluster_id feature  cluster_size  cluster_stability  mutual_information_dev  development_missing_rate is_representative final_action\n        5.0     V17          19.0                1.0                0.008264                  0.140606             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0     V18          19.0                1.0                0.007489                  0.140606             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0     V21          19.0                1.0                0.008000                  0.140606             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0     V22          19.0                1.0                0.005702                  0.140606             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0    V302          19.0                1.0                0.008918                  0.000025             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0    V303          19.0                1.0                0.007506                  0.000025             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0    V304          19.0                1.0                0.008043                  0.000025             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0     V39          19.0                1.0                0.007977                  0.295935             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0     V40          19.0                1.0                0.009075                  0.295935              True         keep\n        5.0     V42          19.0                1.0                0.006458                  0.295935             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0     V43          19.0                1.0                0.007834                  0.295935             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0     V59          19.0                1.0                0.004381                  0.141587             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0     V60          19.0                1.0                0.005535                  0.141587             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0     V63          19.0                1.0                0.006435                  0.141587             False tag_not_drop\n        5.0     V64          19.0                1.0                0.005695                  0.141587             False tag_not_drop\n\nNo dimensionality reduction is FITTED here. Redundancy is a diagnosis; any reducer must be refitted inside each training fold to stay leakage-safe.\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 770x374 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":11},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Interpreting Section 7\n\nThe source notebook grouped V columns **once**, on a pooled development sample,\nwith Pearson correlation and union-find. Two problems: transitive union-find\nchains features that are not mutually correlated, and a single pooled estimate\ncannot distinguish a structural redundancy from one that happens to hold in a\nparticular stretch of time.\n\nMeasuring co-membership across blocks answers a sharper question: *would this\ngrouping still exist if I had looked at a different period?* Only groups that\nsurvive that test earn a representative. Everything else is returned to\n`monitor`, which is an admission of uncertainty rather than a decision.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 8. Entity and Graph Readiness Analysis\n\nFor each available candidate entity column — `card1`–`card6`, `addr1`, `addr2`,\n`P_emaildomain`, `R_emaildomain`, `DeviceInfo`, `DeviceType` — the notebook\ncomputes coverage, cardinality, singleton rate, frequency distribution,\npersistence across blocks, the share of OOT values previously seen in\ndevelopment, cold-start rate, warm/cold transaction counts, fraud prevalence for\neach, the entity-degree distribution and fraud concentration by degree band.\n\n**Cold-start rate is defined twice, and the two are never conflated:**\n\n* *primary* — OOT rows whose non-missing entity value was unseen in development,\n  divided by **OOT rows with a non-missing value for that field**;\n* *secondary* — the same numerator divided by **all OOT rows**, which is smaller\n  whenever the field is sparse.\n\n**Two things this section deliberately does not do.** It does not compose\n`card1 + addr1 + (day − D)` (or any other combination) into a synthetic customer\nidentity — that linkage rule is undocumented for these anonymised columns and\nunvalidated here. And it does not treat a shared identifier as evidence that two\ntransactions belong to the same person; a shared value is a shared *attribute*.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\"\"\"Section 8 - entity and graph readiness, cold start, degree concentration.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef calculate_entity_persistence(frame: pd.DataFrame, column: str,\n                                 block_column: str = \"chrono_block\") -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Coverage, cardinality, singleton rate and cross-block persistence.\n\n    ``persistence_mean_blocks`` is the average number of distinct blocks in\n    which a value appears; ``share_in_all_blocks`` is the share of distinct\n    values seen in every block. Both denominators are distinct non-missing\n    values, not rows.\n    \"\"\"\n    if column not in frame.columns:\n        raise KeyError(f\"Entity column '{column}' is absent.\")\n    values = frame[column]\n    non_missing = values.notna()\n    n_rows = int(len(frame))\n    n_non_missing = int(non_missing.sum())\n    if n_non_missing == 0:\n        return {\"entity\": column, \"assessable\": False,\n                \"reason\": \"no non-missing values\", \"n_rows\": n_rows,\n                \"non_missing_rows\": 0, \"coverage\": 0.0, \"cardinality\": 0,\n                \"singleton_rate\": np.nan, \"persistence_mean_blocks\": np.nan,\n                \"share_in_all_blocks\": np.nan, \"top1_share\": np.nan}\n    counts = values[non_missing].value_counts()\n    per_value_blocks = (frame.loc[non_missing, [column, block_column]]\n                        .groupby(column, observed=True)[block_column].nunique())\n    n_blocks = int(frame[block_column].nunique())\n    return {\n        \"entity\": column, \"assessable\": True, \"reason\": \"\",\n        \"n_rows\": n_rows,\n        \"non_missing_rows\": n_non_missing,\n        \"coverage\": n_non_missing / n_rows,\n        \"cardinality\": int(counts.size),\n        \"singleton_rate\": float((counts == 1).mean()),\n        \"top1_share\": float(counts.iloc[0] / n_non_missing),\n        \"median_frequency\": float(counts.median()),\n        \"p95_frequency\": float(counts.quantile(0.95)),\n        \"persistence_mean_blocks\": float(per_value_blocks.mean()),\n        \"share_in_all_blocks\": float((per_value_blocks == n_blocks).mean()),\n    }\n\n\ndef calculate_cold_start_rate(dev: pd.DataFrame, oot: pd.DataFrame, column: str,\n                              target: str = TARGET_COLUMN) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"OOT cold-start exposure for one entity column, on two denominators.\n\n    Primary definition (the one used in the summary table):\n\n        cold_start_rate = OOT rows whose NON-MISSING value was unseen in\n                          development / OOT rows with a non-missing value.\n\n    Secondary definition, reported alongside and clearly distinguished:\n\n        cold_start_rate_all_oot_rows = the same numerator divided by ALL OOT\n                          rows, so rows where the entity is missing dilute it.\n\n    Fraud prevalence is reported separately for warm-start and cold-start rows\n    with the row counts that produced it. The development value set is the only\n    thing learned here, and it is learned from development rows alone.\n    \"\"\"\n    for frame, label in ((dev, \"dev\"), (oot, \"oot\")):\n        if column not in frame.columns:\n            raise KeyError(f\"Entity column '{column}' absent from the {label} frame.\")\n    seen = set(pd.unique(dev.loc[dev[column].notna(), column]))\n    oot_values = oot[column]\n    non_missing = oot_values.notna()\n    n_oot_rows = int(len(oot))\n    n_non_missing = int(non_missing.sum())\n    if n_non_missing == 0:\n        return {\"entity\": column, \"assessable\": False,\n                \"reason\": \"no non-missing OOT values\", \"n_dev_values_seen\": len(seen),\n                \"oot_rows\": n_oot_rows, \"oot_rows_non_missing\": 0,\n                \"cold_start_rows\": 0, \"warm_start_rows\": 0,\n                \"cold_start_rate\": np.nan, \"cold_start_rate_all_oot_rows\": np.nan,\n                \"fraud_rate_cold_start\": np.nan, \"fraud_rate_warm_start\": np.nan,\n                \"positives_cold_start\": 0, \"positives_warm_start\": 0,\n                \"unseen_value_share\": np.nan}\n    is_cold = non_missing & ~oot_values.isin(seen)\n    is_warm = non_missing & oot_values.isin(seen)\n    y = oot[target]\n    n_cold, n_warm = int(is_cold.sum()), int(is_warm.sum())\n    return {\n        \"entity\": column, \"assessable\": True, \"reason\": \"\",\n        \"n_dev_values_seen\": len(seen),\n        \"oot_rows\": n_oot_rows,\n        \"oot_rows_non_missing\": n_non_missing,\n        \"cold_start_rows\": n_cold,\n        \"warm_start_rows\": n_warm,\n        \"cold_start_rate\": n_cold / n_non_missing,\n        \"cold_start_rate_all_oot_rows\": n_cold / n_oot_rows,\n        \"unseen_value_share\": float(\n            (~pd.Index(pd.unique(oot_values[non_missing])).isin(list(seen))).mean()),\n        \"positives_cold_start\": int(y[is_cold].sum()),\n        \"positives_warm_start\": int(y[is_warm].sum()),\n        \"fraud_rate_cold_start\": float(y[is_cold].mean()) if n_cold else np.nan,\n        \"fraud_rate_warm_start\": float(y[is_warm].mean()) if n_warm else np.nan,\n        \"cold_start_reliable\": n_cold >= CONFIG[\"MIN_GROUP_N\"],\n    }\n\n\ndef summarize_degree_fraud_concentration(frame: pd.DataFrame, column: str,\n                                         target: str = TARGET_COLUMN,\n                                         bands: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 5, 20, 100)) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"Fraud concentration by entity degree band, computed from an aggregated table.\n\n    \"Degree\" here is the transaction count attached to an entity value within\n    the supplied frame - a bipartite transaction-to-entity degree. No dense\n    adjacency matrix is materialised: the whole computation is a value_counts\n    join, which is why it scales to 590k rows.\n    \"\"\"\n    if column not in frame.columns:\n        raise KeyError(f\"Entity column '{column}' is absent.\")\n    non_missing = frame[column].notna()\n    if not non_missing.any():\n        return pd.DataFrame(columns=[\"entity\", \"degree_band\", \"n_rows\", \"positives\",\n                                     \"fraud_rate\", \"share_of_rows\", \"share_of_positives\"])\n    degree = frame.loc[non_missing, column].map(frame.loc[non_missing, column].value_counts())\n    edges = list(bands) + [np.inf]\n    labels = [f\"{edges[i]}-{edges[i + 1] - 1 if np.isfinite(edges[i + 1]) else 'inf'}\"\n              for i in range(len(edges) - 1)]\n    band = pd.cut(degree, bins=[b - 0.5 for b in edges[:-1]] + [np.inf],\n                  labels=labels, right=True)\n    y = frame.loc[non_missing, target]\n    grouped = pd.DataFrame({\"band\": band, \"y\": y}).groupby(\"band\", observed=False)[\"y\"]\n    out = grouped.agg(n_rows=\"size\", positives=\"sum\").reset_index()\n    out[\"fraud_rate\"] = out[\"positives\"] / out[\"n_rows\"].replace(0, np.nan)\n    out[\"share_of_rows\"] = out[\"n_rows\"] / out[\"n_rows\"].sum()\n    out[\"share_of_positives\"] = out[\"positives\"] / max(int(out[\"positives\"].sum()), 1)\n    out.insert(0, \"entity\", column)\n    out = out.rename(columns={\"band\": \"degree_band\"})\n    return out\n\n\nif ENTITY_COLUMNS:\n    AUDITOR.guard(\"entity_seen_value_sets\", dev_df[[ID_COLUMN]])\n    _persistence = [calculate_entity_persistence(dev_df, c) for c in ENTITY_COLUMNS]\n    _cold = [calculate_cold_start_rate(dev_df, oot_df, c) for c in ENTITY_COLUMNS]\n    for column in ENTITY_COLUMNS:\n        AUDITOR.register_fit(f\"seen_values::{column}\", \"category_map\",\n                             dev_df[ID_COLUMN].to_numpy(), DEV_BLOCKS)\n\n    ENTITY_SUMMARY = (pd.DataFrame(_persistence)\n                      .merge(pd.DataFrame(_cold), on=\"entity\", how=\"outer\",\n                             suffixes=(\"_dev\", \"_oot\")))\n    ENTITY_COLD_START_PATH = os.path.join(OUTPUT_DIRECTORY, \"entity_cold_start_summary.csv\")\n    ENTITY_SUMMARY.to_csv(ENTITY_COLD_START_PATH, index=False)\n    LOG.info(\"Wrote %s\", ENTITY_COLD_START_PATH)\n\n    DEGREE_CONCENTRATION = pd.concat(\n        [summarize_degree_fraud_concentration(dev_df, c) for c in ENTITY_COLUMNS],\n        ignore_index=True)\n\n    print(ENTITY_SUMMARY[[\"entity\", \"coverage\", \"cardinality\", \"singleton_rate\",\n                          \"persistence_mean_blocks\", \"share_in_all_blocks\",\n                          \"oot_rows_non_missing\", \"cold_start_rows\",\n                          \"cold_start_rate\", \"cold_start_rate_all_oot_rows\",\n                          \"fraud_rate_cold_start\", \"fraud_rate_warm_start\"]]\n          .round(4).to_string(index=False))\n    print(\"\\nDenominators: coverage and cold_start_rate_all_oot_rows use ALL rows of \"\n          \"their frame; cold_start_rate uses only OOT rows with a non-missing value; \"\n          \"singleton_rate and persistence use distinct values, not rows.\")\n    print(\"\\nInterpretation limits: card/address/email/device columns are anonymised. \"\n          \"A shared value is a shared ATTRIBUTE, not proof of a shared person. No \"\n          \"composite customer identity is constructed here, because no documented \"\n          \"linkage rule has been validated for these columns.\")\n    print(\"\\nFraud concentration by entity degree band (development rows):\")\n    print(DEGREE_CONCENTRATION.round(5).to_string(index=False))\n\n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13, 4))\n    _cs = ENTITY_SUMMARY.dropna(subset=[\"cold_start_rate\"]).sort_values(\"cold_start_rate\")\n    _idx = np.arange(len(_cs))\n    axes[0].barh(_idx - 0.2, _cs.cold_start_rate, height=0.4,\n                 label=\"of OOT rows with a value\")\n    axes[0].barh(_idx + 0.2, _cs.cold_start_rate_all_oot_rows, height=0.4,\n                 label=\"of all OOT rows\")\n    axes[0].set_yticks(_idx)\n    axes[0].set_yticklabels(_cs.entity)\n    axes[0].set_xlabel(\"OOT cold-start rate\")\n    axes[0].set_title(\"Cold-start exposure under both denominators\")\n    axes[0].legend(fontsize=8)\n    for entity, part in DEGREE_CONCENTRATION.groupby(\"entity\"):\n        axes[1].plot(part.degree_band.astype(str), 100 * part.fraud_rate,\n                     marker=\"o\", label=entity)\n    axes[1].set_xlabel(\"entity degree band (transactions per value, development)\")\n    axes[1].set_ylabel(\"fraud rate (%)\")\n    axes[1].set_title(\"Fraud concentration by entity degree\")\n    axes[1].legend(fontsize=7, ncol=2)\n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.show()\nelse:\n    ENTITY_SUMMARY = pd.DataFrame()\n    DEGREE_CONCENTRATION = pd.DataFrame()\n    ENTITY_COLD_START_PATH = None\n    LOG.warning(\"No candidate entity columns present: Section 8 is NOT COMPUTED.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-24T03:40:57.128791Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-24T03:40:57.129167Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-24T03:41:00.637073Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-24T03:40:57.129137Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-24T03:41:00.636061Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"03:40:59 | INFO    | Wrote ./artifacts/entity_cold_start_summary.csv\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stdout","text":"       entity  coverage  cardinality  singleton_rate  persistence_mean_blocks  share_in_all_blocks  oot_rows_non_missing  cold_start_rows  cold_start_rate  cold_start_rate_all_oot_rows  fraud_rate_cold_start  fraud_rate_warm_start\n   DeviceInfo    0.2107         1639          0.2550                   2.1818               0.2282                 19130              374           0.0196                        0.0032                 0.2005                 0.0866\n   DeviceType    0.2490            2          0.0000                   4.0000               1.0000                 23172                0           0.0000                        0.0000                    NaN                 0.0941\nP_emaildomain    0.8442           59          0.0000                   4.0000               1.0000                 97238                0           0.0000                        0.0000                    NaN                 0.0357\nR_emaildomain    0.2405           60          0.0000                   3.8500               0.8833                 23671                0           0.0000                        0.0000                    NaN                 0.0935\n        addr1    0.8862          328          0.3780                   1.8659               0.2134                106163                7           0.0001                        0.0001                 0.0000                 0.0231\n        addr2    0.8862           70          0.2571                   1.7429               