     2-4       0          0         NaN        0.00000             0.00000\nP_emaildomain        5-19       0          0         NaN        0.00000             0.00000\nP_emaildomain       20-99     525         36     0.06857        0.00132             0.00250\nP_emaildomain     100-inf  398321      14368     0.03607        0.99868             0.99750\nR_emaildomain         1-1       0          0         NaN        0.00000             0.00000\nR_emaildomain         2-4       0          0         NaN        0.00000             0.00000\nR_emaildomain        5-19      53          1     0.01887        0.00047             0.00011\nR_emaildomain       20-99    1139         53     0.04653        0.01002             0.00588\nR_emaildomain     100-inf  112428       8959     0.07969        0.98951             0.99401\n   DeviceInfo         1-1     418         15     0.03589        0.00420             0.00217\n   DeviceInfo         2-4    1255         70     0.05578        0.01261             0.01013\n   DeviceInfo        5-19    4502        518     0.11506        0.04523             0.07499\n   DeviceInfo       20-99    9512       1377     0.14476        0.09556             0.19933\n   DeviceInfo     100-inf   83849       4928     0.05877        0.84240             0.71338\n   DeviceType         1-1       0          0         NaN        0.00000             0.00000\n   DeviceType         2-4       0          0         NaN        0.00000             0.00000\n   DeviceType        5-19       0          0         NaN        0.00000             0.00000\n   DeviceType       20-99       0          0         NaN        0.00000             0.00000\n   DeviceType     100-inf  117638       9030     0.07676        1.00000             1.00000\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1430x440 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":12},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Interpreting Section 8\n\nCold-start exposure is the single most consequential number for a graph-tabular\nmodel. Any encoding built from an entity's history — frequency, running mean,\nnode embedding, degree — is **undefined** for a value first seen in the holdout,\nand the share of the holdout in that position bounds how much of the graph story\ncan transfer at all. High singleton rates carry the same warning at the value\nlevel: an entity seen once contributes a degree-1 node with no neighbourhood to\naggregate over.\n\nDegree bands are computed from an aggregated `value_counts` table. No dense\nadjacency matrix is materialised anywhere, which is what keeps this affordable at\n590k rows and is the pattern any later graph pre-analysis should follow.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 9. Block-Count Sensitivity Analysis\n\nThe 5-block split is a choice. If the conclusions depend on it, they are\nartefacts. The key drift diagnostics are recomputed at 4, 5, 6 and 8 blocks;\neach configuration reserves its own final chronological block as OOT and\n**re-learns every reference statistic from its own development rows**. Nothing —\nbin edges, cut points, fitted objects — is reused across configurations, because\nan object learned under one split is not a development-only object under another.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\"\"\"Section 9 - block-count sensitivity of the drift conclusions.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef run_block_sensitivity(frame: pd.DataFrame, features: Sequence[str],\n                          block_counts: Sequence[int] = CONFIG[\"SENSITIVITY_BLOCK_COUNTS\"],\n                          time_column: str = TIME_COLUMN,\n                          id_column: str = ID_COLUMN) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"Recompute the key drift flags under several block-count configurations.\n\n    For each configuration the split is rebuilt from scratch, the final\n    chronological block becomes that configuration's OOT, and every reference\n    statistic - bin edges, decile cut point, verdict rule input - is re-learned\n    from that configuration's development rows only. Nothing is reused across\n    configurations, because a bin edge learned under a 5-block split is not a\n    development-only object under an 8-block split.\n\n    Returns:\n        Long DataFrame: one row per (n_blocks, feature) with psi_max_dev,\n        spearman_corr_time, lift_stability and the resulting verdict.\n    \"\"\"\n    rows = []\n    for n_blocks in block_counts:\n        labels = create_temporal_blocks(frame, n_blocks, time_column, id_column)\n        local = frame[[id_column, time_column, \"elapsed_days\"] + list(features)].copy()\n        local[\"chrono_block\"] = labels\n        local[TARGET_COLUMN] = frame[TARGET_COLUMN]\n        dev_blocks = list(range(1, n_blocks))\n        local_dev = local[local[\"chrono_block\"] < n_blocks]\n        local_auditor = LeakageAuditor(\n            oot_ids=local.loc[local[\"chrono_block\"] == n_blocks, id_column].to_numpy(),\n            oot_blocks=[n_blocks])\n        local_auditor.guard(f\"sensitivity_{n_blocks}_blocks\", local_dev[[id_column]])\n\n        spearman = calculate_spearman_time_risk(local_dev, features).set_index(\"feature\")\n        for feature in features:\n            reference = local_dev.loc[local_dev[\"chrono_block\"] == dev_blocks[0], feature]\n            edges = learn_psi_bins(reference)\n            psis = []\n            for block in dev_blocks[1:]:\n                psis.append(calculate_psi(\n                    reference, local_dev.loc[local_dev[\"chrono_block\"] == block, feature],\n                    edges=edges)[\"psi\"])\n            psis = [p for p in psis if np.isfinite(p)]\n            psi_max = max(psis) if psis else np.nan\n\n            values = local_dev[feature]\n            values = values[pd.to_numeric(values, errors=\"coerce\").map(np.isfinite)]\n            cut = (float(np.quantile(values, CONFIG[\"TOP_DECILE_QUANTILE\"]))\n                   if len(values) >= CONFIG[\"MIN_NON_MISSING\"] else float(\"nan\"))\n            lifts = {}\n            for block in dev_blocks:\n                result = calculate_top_decile_lift(\n                    local.loc[local[\"chrono_block\"] == block, [feature, TARGET_COLUMN]],\n                    feature, cut)\n                if result[\"assessable\"]:\n                    lifts[block] = result[\"lift\"]\n            stability = calculate_lift_stability(lifts)\n\n            corr = spearman.loc[feature, \"spearman_corr_time\"] if feature in spearman.index else np.nan\n            band = classify_psi(psi_max)\n            strong_time = np.isfinite(corr) and abs(corr) >= CONFIG[\"SPEARMAN_RISK_THRESHOLD\"]\n            unstable = (stability[\"lift_stability\"] is not None\n                        and stability[\"lift_stability\"] < CONFIG[\"LIFT_STABILITY_THRESHOLD\"])\n            if not np.isfinite(psi_max) and not np.isfinite(corr):\n                verdict = \"insufficient_evidence\"\n            elif band == \"severe_drift\" or (strong_time and unstable):\n                verdict = \"temporal_risk\"\n            elif band == \"moderate_drift\" or strong_time or unstable:\n                verdict = \"moderate_risk\"\n            else:\n                verdict = \"stable\"\n\n            rows.append({\"n_blocks\": n_blocks, \"oot_block\": n_blocks, \"feature\": feature,\n                         \"psi_max_dev\": psi_max, \"psi_band\": band,\n                         \"spearman_corr_time\": corr,\n                         \"lift_stability\": stability[\"lift_stability\"],\n                         \"n_lift_blocks\": stability[\"n_blocks\"], \"verdict\": verdict})\n        LOG.info(\"Sensitivity configuration with %d blocks complete \"\n                 \"(development blocks 1-%d, OOT block %d).\", n_blocks, n_blocks - 1, n_blocks)\n    return pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\n\n# Restrict the sensitivity sweep to the families whose verdicts drive the\n# protocol (D-series plus the amount) and any feature currently flagged, which\n# keeps the four re-splits affordable without cherry-picking stable features.\nSENSITIVITY_FEATURES = sorted(set(\n    [c for c in AUDIT_FEATURES if family_of(c) in {\"D\", \"amount\", \"distance\"}]\n    + feature_risk.loc[feature_risk.verdict.isin([\"temporal_risk\", \"moderate_risk\"]),\n                       \"feature\"].tolist()\n))\nprint(f\"Workload estimate: {len(CONFIG['SENSITIVITY_BLOCK_COUNTS'])} configurations x \"\n      f\"{len(SENSITIVITY_FEATURES)} features, each re-splitting {len(df):,} rows.\")\n\nBLOCK_SENSITIVITY = run_block_sensitivity(df, SENSITIVITY_FEATURES)\n\n_baseline = BLOCK_SENSITIVITY[BLOCK_SENSITIVITY.n_blocks == N_BLOCKS][[\"feature\", \"verdict\"]] \\\n    .rename(columns={\"verdict\": \"verdict_baseline\"})\nBLOCK_SENSITIVITY = BLOCK_SENSITIVITY.merge(_baseline, on=\"feature\", how=\"left\")\nBLOCK_SENSITIVITY[\"verdict_changed_vs_baseline\"] = (\n    BLOCK_SENSITIVITY[\"verdict\"] != BLOCK_SENSITIVITY[\"verdict_baseline\"])\n\n_agreement = (BLOCK_SENSITIVITY.groupby(\"feature\")[\"verdict\"].nunique()\n              .rename(\"distinct_verdicts\").reset_index())\n_agreement[\"robust_to_block_count\"] = _agreement[\"distinct_verdicts\"] == 1\nBLOCK_SENSITIVITY = BLOCK_SENSITIVITY.merge(_agreement, on=\"feature\", how=\"left\")\n\nBLOCK_SENSITIVITY_PATH = os.path.join(OUTPUT_DIRECTORY, \"block_sensitivity_summary.csv\")\nBLOCK_SENSITIVITY.to_csv(BLOCK_SENSITIVITY_PATH, index=False)\nLOG.info(\"Wrote %s\", BLOCK_SENSITIVITY_PATH)\n\n_unstable = _agreement[~_agreement.robust_to_block_count][\"feature\"].tolist()\nprint(f\"\\nFeatures whose verdict is identical under 4, 5, 6 and 8 blocks: \"\n      f\"{int(_agreement.robust_to_block_count.sum())} of {len(_agreement)}.\")\nprint(f\"Features whose verdict changes with the block count: \"\n      f\"{_unstable if _unstable else 'none'}\")\nprint(\"\\nVerdict counts by configuration:\")\nprint(BLOCK_SENSITIVITY.pivot_table(index=\"verdict\", columns=\"n_blocks\",\n                                    values=\"feature\", aggfunc=\"count\", fill_value=0)\n      .to_string())\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 3.6))\n_pivot = BLOCK_SENSITIVITY.pivot_table(index=\"n_blocks\", columns=\"verdict\",\n                                       values=\"feature\", aggfunc=\"count\", fill_value=0)\n_pivot.plot(kind=\"bar\", stacked=True, ax=ax)\nax.set_xlabel(\"number of chronological blocks (final block reserved as OOT)\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"features\")\nax.set_title(\"Are the drift verdicts an artefact of the block count?\")\nax.legend(fontsize=8, title=\"verdict\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-24T03:42:27.992271Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-24T03:42:27.992886Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-24T03:56:05.567929Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-24T03:42:27.992846Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-24T03:56:05.566741Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Workload estimate: 4 configurations x 282 features, each re-splitting 590,540 rows.\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stderr","text":"03:45:33 | INFO    | Sensitivity configuration with 4 blocks complete (development blocks 1-3, OOT block 4).\n03:48:51 | INFO    | Sensitivity configuration with 5 blocks complete (development blocks 1-4, OOT block 5).\n03:52:21 | INFO    | Sensitivity configuration with 6 blocks complete (development blocks 1-5, OOT block 6).\n03:56:05 | INFO    | Sensitivity configuration with 8 blocks complete (development blocks 1-7, OOT block 8).\n03:56:05 | INFO    | Wrote ./artifacts/block_sensitivity_summary.csv\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stdout","text":"\nFeatures whose verdict is identical under 4, 5, 6 and 8 blocks: 148 of 282.\nFeatures whose verdict changes with the block count: ['C10', 'C8', 'D1', 'D14', 'D2', 'D3', 'TransactionAmt', 'V167', 'V168', 'V169', 'V170', 'V171', 'V172', 'V173', 'V174', 'V175', 'V176', 'V177', 'V178', 'V179', 'V180', 'V181', 'V182', 'V183', 'V184', 'V185', 'V186', 