e by: Spearman (the association is monotone, not linear, and\nthe tails are heavy), the full empirical distribution of `|ρ|` across development\nfeatures, and a quantile-derived alternative cut-off computed from development\nrows only. The configured value remains configurable and is fixed before any OOT\nnumber is consulted.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\"\"\"Section 10 - deriving the temporal-correlation threshold instead of asserting it.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef derive_correlation_threshold(correlations: Sequence[float],\n                                 quantile: float = 0.99) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Derive a temporal-correlation cut-off from the DEVELOPMENT distribution.\n\n    The source notebook used ``DRIFT_CORR = 0.30`` on a Pearson correlation with\n    no stated provenance. Two changes are made here:\n\n      1. Spearman replaces Pearson, because the association of interest is\n         monotone drift, not a linear relationship, and the D and V series have\n         tails that dominate a Pearson estimate.\n      2. The cut-off becomes an EMPIRICAL quantile of the observed |Spearman|\n         distribution across development features, so it is a statement about\n         this dataset (\"the most time-associated 1% of features\") rather than an\n         imported constant. It is computed from development rows only.\n\n    Returns the derived value alongside the configured one so the difference is\n    visible and the choice remains the analyst's.\n    \"\"\"\n    values = np.asarray([abs(c) for c in correlations if c is not None and np.isfinite(c)])\n    if values.size == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"No finite correlations supplied; a threshold cannot be derived.\")\n    return {\n        \"n_features\": int(values.size),\n        \"quantile\": quantile,\n        \"derived_threshold\": float(np.quantile(values, quantile)),\n        \"median_abs_corr\": float(np.median(values)),\n        \"p90_abs_corr\": float(np.quantile(values, 0.90)),\n        \"max_abs_corr\": float(values.max()),\n        \"configured_threshold\": CONFIG[\"SPEARMAN_RISK_THRESHOLD\"],\n        \"share_above_configured\": float((values >= CONFIG[\"SPEARMAN_RISK_THRESHOLD\"]).mean()),\n    }\n\n\nAUDITOR.guard(\"correlation_threshold_derivation\", dev_df[[ID_COLUMN]])\nTHRESHOLD_EVIDENCE = derive_correlation_threshold(\n    feature_risk[\"spearman_corr_time\"].tolist())\nAUDITOR.register_fit(\"temporal_correlation_threshold\", \"threshold\",\n                     dev_df[ID_COLUMN].to_numpy(), DEV_BLOCKS)\n\nprint(json.dumps(THRESHOLD_EVIDENCE, indent=2))\nprint(\"\\nReading this table: the configured threshold \"\n      f\"({CONFIG['SPEARMAN_RISK_THRESHOLD']}) flags \"\n      f\"{100 * THRESHOLD_EVIDENCE['share_above_configured']:.1f}% of assessable \"\n      \"development features. The empirically derived cut-off is the \"\n      f\"{THRESHOLD_EVIDENCE['quantile']:.0%} quantile of the same distribution. \"\n      \"Neither is a law: a threshold sets how much review capacity is spent, and \"\n      \"it is deliberately fixed BEFORE any OOT number is consulted.\")\nprint(\"\\nStatistical significance is not practical risk. At ~470k development rows \"\n      \"a |Spearman| of 0.01 is significant at any conventional alpha and is \"\n      \"practically irrelevant, which is why the verdict rule combines the \"\n      \"correlation with PSI and with lift stability rather than relying on any \"\n      \"single cut-off.\")\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 3.6))\n_abs_corr = feature_risk[\"spearman_corr_time\"].abs().dropna()\naxes[0].hist(_abs_corr, bins=40)\naxes[0].axvline(CONFIG[\"SPEARMAN_RISK_THRESHOLD\"], color=\"crimson\", ls=\"--\",\n                label=f\"configured {CONFIG['SPEARMAN_RISK_THRESHOLD']}\")\naxes[0].axvline(THRESHOLD_EVIDENCE[\"derived_threshold\"], color=\"seagreen\", ls=\"--\",\n                label=f\"derived p{THRESHOLD_EVIDENCE['quantile']:.0%} \"\n                      f\"{THRESHOLD_EVIDENCE['derived_threshold']:.3f}\")\naxes[0].set_xlabel(\"|Spearman correlation with elapsed time| (development)\")\naxes[0].set_ylabel(\"features\")\naxes[0].set_title(\"Distribution of temporal association across development features\")\naxes[0].legend(fontsize=8)\n\n_sorted = np.sort(_abs_corr)\naxes[1].plot(_sorted, np.arange(1, _sorted.size + 1) / _sorted.size)\naxes[1].axvline(CONFIG[\"SPEARMAN_RISK_THRESHOLD\"], color=\"crimson\", ls=\"--\")\naxes[1].axvline(THRESHOLD_EVIDENCE[\"derived_threshold\"], color=\"seagreen\", ls=\"--\")\naxes[1].set_xlabel(\"|Spearman correlation with elapsed time|\")\naxes[1].set_ylabel(\"cumulative share of features\")\naxes[1].set_title(\"How many features any given cut-off would flag\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Interpreting Section 10\n\nA threshold is a **capacity decision**: it determines how many features get human\nreview, and the honest way to set it is against the observed distribution, not\nagainst a number inherited from a blog post. Two guarantees hold regardless of\nthe value chosen: it is derived from development data only, and PSI and lift\nstability are carried alongside it, so no feature is condemned or exonerated by a\nsingle statistic.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 11. Machine-Readable Decisions Ledger\n\nOne JSON entry per feature: family, the ten diagnostic values (with `null`\npreserved wherever a metric was not applicable or not estimable), verdict,\nreason, action, decision scope, the evidence sections that produced it, the\nconfiguration snapshot, the data hash, the code version and a timestamp.\n\n`update_decisions_ledger` enforces the contract instead of trusting it: the\nverdict and action vocabularies are closed, a non-empty reason is mandatory, and\nat least one evidence section must be cited. A decision cannot enter the ledger\nwithout its evidence.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\"\"\"Section 11 - the machine-readable decisions ledger.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _nullable(value: Any) -> Optional[float]:\n    \"\"\"Convert numpy/NaN values to JSON-safe floats, preserving null as null.\"\"\"\n    if value is None:\n        return None\n    if isinstance(value, (np.floating, float)):\n        return None if not np.isfinite(value) else float(value)\n    if isinstance(value, (np.integer, int)):\n        return int(value)\n    if isinstance(value, (np.bool_, bool)):\n        return bool(value)\n    return value\n\n\ndef update_decisions_ledger(ledger: Dict[str, Dict[str, Any]], feature: str,\n                            feature_family: str, diagnostics: Mapping[str, Any],\n                            verdict: str, reason: str, action: str,\n                            evidence_sections: Sequence[str],\n                            config_snapshot: Mapping[str, Any],\n                            data_hash: str, code_version: str,\n                            decision_scope: str = \"development_only\") -> Dict[str, Dict[str, Any]]:\n    \"\"\"Insert or replace one feature's entry in the decisions ledger.\n\n    Contract enforced here rather than trusted:\n      * ``verdict`` and ``action`` must be drawn from the allowed vocabularies;\n      * a metric that was not computed stays ``null`` - it is never coerced to 0;\n      * a non-empty ``reason`` and at least one evidence section are required for\n        every verdict, so no decision can be recorded without its evidence.\n    \"\"\"\n    allowed_verdicts = {\"stable\", \"moderate_risk\", \"temporal_risk\", \"redundant\",\n                        \"insufficient_evidence\"}\n    allowed_actions = {\"keep\", \"drop\", \"tag_not_drop\", \"monitor\", \"investigate\"}\n    allowed_scopes = {\"development_only\", \"oot_evaluation_only\", \"development_plus_oot_evaluation\"}\n    if verdict not in allowed_verdicts:\n        raise ValueError(f\"verdict '{verdict}' is not one of {sorted(allowed_verdicts)}.\")\n    if action not in allowed_actions:\n        raise ValueError(f\"action '{action}' is not one of {sorted(allowed_actions)}.\")\n    if decision_scope not in allowed_scopes:\n        raise ValueError(f\"decision_scope {decision_scope!r} is not one of {sorted(allowed_scopes)}.\")\n    if not reason:\n        raise ValueError(f\"A reason is mandatory; '{feature}' supplied none.\")\n    if not evidence_sections:\n        raise ValueError(f\"'{feature}' must cite at least one evidence section.\")\n\n    ledger[feature] = {\n        \"feature_family\": feature_family,\n        \"diagnostic_results\": {\n            \"psi_max\": _nullable(diagnostics.get(\"psi_max\")),\n            \"ks_stat\": _nullable(diagnostics.get(\"ks_stat\")),\n            \"ks_adjusted_pvalue\": _nullable(diagnostics.get(\"ks_adjusted_pvalue\")),\n            \"spearman_corr_time\": _nullable(diagnostics.get(\"spearman_corr_time\")),\n            \"lift_stability\": _nullable(diagnostics.get(\"lift_stability\")),\n            \"missingness_drift\": _nullable(diagnostics.get(\"missingness_drift\")),\n            \"cluster_id\": _nullable(diagnostics.get(\"cluster_id\")),\n            \"cluster_stability\": _nullable(diagnostics.get(\"cluster_stability\")),\n            \"mutual_information_dev\": _nullable(diagnostics.get(\"mutual_information_dev\")),\n            \"oot_cold_start_rate\": _nullable(diagnostics.get(\"oot_cold_start_rate\")),\n        },\n        \"verdict\": verdict,\n        \"reason\": reason,\n        \"action\": action,\n        \"decision_scope\": decision_scope,\n        \"evidence_sections\": list(evidence_sections),\n        \"config_snapshot\": dict(config_snapshot),\n        \"data_hash\": data_hash,\n        \"code_version\": code_version,\n        \"created_at\": datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec=\"seconds\"),\n    }\n    return ledger\n\n\nCONFIG_SNAPSHOT = {k: CONFIG[k] for k in [\n    \"RANDOM_SEED\", \"N_BLOCKS\", \"OOT_BLOCK\", \"PSI_BINS\", \"PSI_EPSILON\",\n    \"PSI_MODERATE_THRESHOLD\", \"PSI_SEVERE_THRESHOLD\", \"KS_ALPHA\",\n    \"TEMPORAL_CORRELATION_METHOD\", \"SPEARMAN_RISK_THRESHOLD\",\n    \"CLUSTER_DISTANCE_THRESHOLD\", \"CLUSTER_STABILITY_THRESHOLD\",\n    \"TOP_DECILE_QUANTILE\", \"MIN_POSITIVES_FOR_LIFT\", \"LIFT_STABILITY_THRESHOLD\",\n    \"ENTITY_COLD_START_MODERATE_THRESHOLD\", \"ENTITY_COLD_START_HIGH_THRESHOLD\",\n    \"FIXED_FPR\"]}\n\nDECISIONS_LEDGER: Dict[str, Dict[str, Any]] = {}\n_cluster_lookup = (V_SUMMARY.set_index(\"feature\") if len(V_SUMMARY) else pd.DataFrame())\n_cold_lookup = (ENTITY_SUMMARY.set_index(\"entity\")[\"cold_start_rate\"].to_dict()\n                if len(ENTITY_SUMMARY) else {})\n\nfor _, row in feature_risk.iterrows():\n    feature = row[\"feature\"]\n    verdict, action, reason = row[\"verdict\"], row[\"action\"], row[\"reason\"]\n    sections = [\"5. D-Series Temporal Risk Analysis\"]\n    cluster_id = cluster_stability_value = mi_value = None\n\n    if len(_cluster_lookup) and feature in _cluster_lookup.index:\n        entry = _cluster_lookup.loc[feature]\n        cluster_id = entry.get(\"cluster_id\")\n        cluster_stability_value = entry.get(\"cluster_stability\")\n        mi_value = entry.get(\"mutual_information_dev\")\n        sections.append(\"7. V-Series Redundancy and Stability\")\n        if entry.get(\"final_action\") == \"tag_not_drop\" and verdict == \"stable\":\n            verdict = \"redundant\"\n            action = \"tag_not_drop\"\n            reason = (f\"co-clusters with {entry.get('partners', '')} at distance \"\n                      f\"<= {CONFIG['CLUSTER_DISTANCE_THRESHOLD']} in \"\n                      f\"{entry.get('n_blocks_assessed', 0)} development blocks \"\n                      f\"(stability {entry.get('cluster_stability')}); \"\n                      f\"{entry.get('selection_reason', '')}\")\n\n    sensitivity_rows = BLOCK_SENSITIVITY[BLOCK_SENSITIVITY.feature == feature]\n    if len(sensitivity_rows):\n        sections.append(\"9. Block-Count Sensitivity Analysis\")\n        if not bool(sensitivity_rows[\"robust_to_block_count\"].iloc[0]):\n            reason += (\"; verdict is NOT robust to the block count \"\n                       f\"({sorted(set(sensitivity_rows.verdict))} under \"\n                       f\"{sorted(set(sensitivity_rows.n_blocks))} blocks)\")\n            if action == \"keep\":\n                action = \"monitor\"\n\n    DECISIONS_LEDGER = update_decisions_ledger(\n        DECISIONS_LEDGER, feature=feature, feature_family=row[\"family\"],\n        diagnostics={\n            \"psi_max\": row[\"PSI_max_development_only\"],\n            \"ks_stat\": row[\"ks_stat\"],\n            \"ks_adjusted_pvalue\": row[\"ks_adjusted_pvalue\"],\n            \"spearman_corr_time\": row[\"spearman_corr_time\"],\n            \"lift_stability\": row[\"lift_stability\"],\n            \"missingness_drift\": row[\"missingness_drift\"],\n            \"cluster_id\": cluster_id,\n            \"cluster_stability\": cluster_stability_value,\n            \"mutual_information_dev\": mi_value,\n            \"oot_cold_start_rate\": None,\n        },\n        verdict=verdict, reason=reason, action=action,\n        evidence_sections=sections, config_snapshot=CONFIG_SNAPSHOT,\n        data_hash=DATA_HASH[\"sha256\"], code_version=CONFIG[\"CODE_VERSION\"])\n\nfor entity in ENTITY_COLUMNS:\n    if entity in DECISIONS_LEDGER:\n        DECISIONS_LEDGER[entity][\"diagnostic_results\"][\"oot_cold_start_rate\"] = \\\n            _nullable(_cold_lookup.get(entity))\n        DECISIONS_LEDGER[entity][\"evidence_sections\"].append(\n            \"8. Entity and Graph Readiness Analysis\")\n        DECISIONS_LEDGER[entity][\"decision_scope\"] = \"development_plus_oot_evaluation\"\n        continue\n    rate = _cold_lookup.get(entity)\n    reliable = bool(ENTITY_SUMMARY.set_index(\"entity\").loc[entity, \"cold_start_reliable\"]) \\\n        if len(ENTITY_SUMMARY) and \"cold_start_reliable\" in ENTITY_SUMMARY.columns else False\n    if rate is None or not np.isfinite(rate):\n        verdict, action = \"insufficient_evidence\", \"investigate\"\n        reason = \"no non-missing OOT values, so cold-start exposure is undefined\"\n    elif rate >= CONFIG[\"ENTITY_COLD_START_HIGH_THRESHOLD\"]:\n        verdict, action = \"temporal_risk\", \"tag_not_drop\"\n        reason = (f\"{rate:.1%} of OOT rows with a non-missing value carry an entity \"\n                  \"value unseen in development; history-based encodings of this \"\n                  \"column will be undefined for those rows\")\n    elif rate >= CONFIG[\"ENTITY_COLD_START_MODERATE_THRESHOLD\"]:\n        verdict, action = \"moderate_risk\", \"monitor\"\n        reason = (f\"cold-start rate {rate:.1%} of OOT rows with a value; usable, but \"\n                  \"any frequency or history encoding needs an explicit unseen-value rule\")\n    else:\n        verdict, action = \"stable\", \"keep\"\n        reason = f\"cold-start rate {rate:.1%} of OOT rows with a value\"\n    if not reliable and verdict != \"insufficient_evidence\":\n        reason += (f\"; fewer than {CONFIG['MIN_GROUP_N']} cold-start rows, so the \"\n                   \"cold-start fraud rate is reported but not relied upon\")\n    DECISIONS_LEDGER = update_decisions_ledger(\n        DECISIONS_LEDGER, feature=entity, feature_family=\"entity\",\n        diagnostics={\"oot_cold_start_rate\": rate},\n        verdict=verdict, reason=reason, action=action,\n        evidence_sections=[\"8. Entity and Graph Readiness Analysis\"],\n        config_snapshot=CONFIG_SNAPSHOT, data_hash=DATA_HASH[\"sha256\"],\n        code_version=CONFIG[\"CODE_VERSION\"],\n        decision_scope=\"oot_evaluation_only\")\n\nLEDGER_PATH = os.path.join(OUTPUT_DIRECTORY, \"decisions_ledger.json\")\n_serialised = json.dumps(DECISIONS_LEDGER, indent=2, allow_nan=False)\nwith open(LEDGER_PATH, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n    handle.write(_serialised)\nLOG.info(\"Wrote %s (%d features).\", LEDGER_PATH, len(DECISIONS_LEDGER))\n\n_ledger_frame = pd.DataFrame([\n    {\"feature\": k, \"family\": v[\"feature_family\"], \"verdict\": v[\"verdict\"],\n     \"action\": v[\"action\"]} for k, v in DECISIONS_LEDGER.items()])\nprint(_ledger_frame.groupby([\"verdict\", \"action\"]).size().rename(\"features\")\n      .reset_index().to_string(index=False))\nprint(\"\\nExample entry:\")\nprint(json.dumps(DECISIONS_LEDGER[_ledger_frame.feature.iloc[0]], indent=2)[:1200])\nprint(\"\\n'tag_not_drop' means the column is retained and carried into modelling with \"\n      \"a risk tag attached; it is NOT an automatic exclusion. 'drop' is reserved for \"\n      \"columns with no usable content at all.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Interpreting Section 11\n\n`tag_not_drop` is the deliberate middle path and the reason the action vocabulary\nis not binary. It means: keep the column, carry the risk tag into modelling, and\ntest its removal as an **ablation** rather than assuming the removal is an\nimprovement. Dropping a temporally unstable feature is itself a modelling\ndecision with a cost, and this notebook does not have the evidence to make it.\n\n`insufficient_evidence` is recorded explicitly for the same reason. An unrunnable\ndiagnostic is a fact about the data — a constant column, a near-empty block, too\nfew positives — and hiding it behind a default value would misrepresent the\naudit's coverage.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 12. Consolidated Findings\n\nAssembled from the computed artifacts, with observed evidence, configured\ndecision rules, methodological interpretation and forward-looking\nrecommendations kept in separate blocks so they cannot be read as one another.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\"\"\"Section 12 - consolidated findings, assembled from computed objects only.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef build_findings(feature_risk: pd.DataFrame, checks: pd.DataFrame,\n                   sensitivity: pd.DataFrame, entity_summary: pd.DataFrame,\n                   v_summary: pd.DataFrame, missing_tests: pd.DataFrame,\n                   confounding: pd.DataFrame) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Collect the headline counts. Every value is read back from a computed\n    artifact; nothing here is typed in by hand.\"\"\"\n    verdicts = feature_risk[\"verdict\"].value_counts().to_dict()\n    findings: Dict[str, Any] = {\n        \"leakage_checks_passed\": int(checks[\"passed\"].sum()) if len(checks) else 0,\n        \"leakage_checks_failed\": int((~checks[\"passed\"]).sum()) if len(checks) else 0,\n        \"failed_check_names\": checks.loc[~checks[\"passed\"], \"check\"].tolist() if len(checks) else [],\n        \"features_assessed\": int(len(feature_risk)),\n        \"verdict_counts\": {k: int(v) for k, v in verdicts.items()},\n        \"severe_psi_features\": feature_risk.loc[\n            feature_risk.PSI_band_development_only == \"severe_drift\", \"feature\"].tolist(),\n        \"moderate_psi_features\": feature_risk.loc[\n            feature_risk.PSI_band_development_only == \"moderate_drift\", \"feature\"].tolist(),\n        \"unstable_missingness_patterns\": (\n            confounding.loc[confounding.flag == \"sign_reverses_in_some_strata\",\n                            \"pattern_lead\"].tolist() if len(confounding) else []),\n        \"missingness_patterns_supported\": (\n            int(missing_tests.keep_indicator.sum()) if len(missing_tests) else 0),\n        \"missingness_patterns_tested\": (\n            int(missing_tests.tested.sum()) if len(missing_tests) else 0),\n        \"stable_v_clusters\": (\n            int(v_summary.loc[v_summary.get(\"stable_cluster\", pd.Series(dtype=bool))\n                              .fillna(False), \"cluster_id\"].nunique()) if len(v_summary) else 0),\n        \"v_representatives_kept\": (\n            int(v_summary.get(\"is_representative\", pd.Series(dtype=bool)).fillna(False).sum())\n            if len(v_summary) else 0),\n        \"entity_columns_assessed\": (entity_summary[\"entity\"].tolist()\n                                    if len(entity_summary) else []),\n        \"max_oot_cold_start_rate\": (\n            float(entity_summary[\"cold_start_rate\"].max()) if len(entity_summary) else None),\n        \"features_robust_to_block_count\": (\n            int(sensitivity.drop_duplicates(\"feature\")[\"robust_to_block_count\"].sum())\n            if len(sensitivity) else 0),\n        \"features_sensitive_to_block_count\": (\n            sensitivity.loc[~sensitivity.robust_to_block_count, \"feature\"].unique().tolist()\n            if len(sensitivity) else []),\n    }\n    return findings\n\n\nFINDINGS = build_findings(feature_risk, CHECKS, BLOCK_SENSITIVITY, ENTITY_SUMMARY,\n                          V_SUMMARY, MISSINGNESS_TESTS, CONFOUNDING)\n\nprint(\"=== OBSERVED EVIDENCE (computed in this run) ===\")\nprint(json.dumps(\n    {k: (v if not isinstance(v, list) or len(v) <= 12 else v[:12] + [\"...\"])\n     for k, v in FINDINGS.items()}, indent=2))\n\nprint(\"\"\"\n=== CONFIGURED DECISION RULES (chosen, not discovered) ===\n  PSI bands            stable < {psi_mod}, moderate <= {psi_sev}, severe above\n  KS multiplicity      Benjamini-Hochberg at alpha = {alpha}\n  Temporal association Spearman, flag at |rho| >= {rho} (Section 10 shows the\n                       empirical distribution this cut-off sits in)\n  Lift stability       1 - coefficient of variation, \"stable\" at >= 0.75, and only\n                       estimated with >= {minblocks} blocks and >= {minpos} positives\n  Cluster redundancy   distance 1 - |Spearman| <= {cdist}, co-membership >= {cstab}\n                       across development blocks\n  Entity cold start    moderate at >= 5%, high at >= 20% of OOT rows with a value\n\"\"\".format(psi_mod=CONFIG[\"PSI_MODERATE_THRESHOLD\"], psi_sev=CONFIG[\"PSI_SEVERE_THRESHOLD\"],\n           alpha=CONFIG[\"KS_ALPHA\"], rho=CONFIG[\"SPEARMAN_RISK_THRESHOLD\"],\n           minblocks=CONFIG[\"LIFT_STABILITY_MIN_BLOCKS\"], minpos=CONFIG[\"MIN_POSITIVES_FOR_LIFT\"],\n           cdist=CONFIG[\"CLUSTER_DISTANCE_THRESHOLD\"], cstab=CONFIG[\"CLUSTER_STABILITY_THRESHOLD\"]))\n\nprint(\"\"\"=== METHODOLOGICAL INTERPRETATION ===\n  * Every fitted object above - bin edges, decile cut points, cluster maps,\n    mutual-information scores, the correlation threshold, the seen-value sets -\n    is registered in the leakage auditor with the blocks it was fitted on. The\n    registry, printed in the run manifest, is the auditable form of the claim\n    that OOT was quarantined.\n  * OOT appears as an evaluation-only comparison sample and as the population\n    whose cold-start exposure is measured. Development feature verdicts do not\n    read OOT statistics. Entity cold-start classifications are explicitly marked\n    decision_scope=oot_evaluation_only and must not be represented as development-only.\n  * Association is not causation, and predictive lift is not an effect. A\n    missingness indicator that separates fraud may be recording a failed join\n    rather than customer behaviour; the ProductCD stratification in Section 6\n    is what distinguishes the two, not the aggregate contrast.\n  * Shared card, address, email-domain or device values are shared ATTRIBUTES.\n    They do not establish that two transactions belong to one person, and no\n    composite identity is built here.\n\n=== RECOMMENDATIONS FOR LATER MODELLING (not results) ===\n  1. Forward-chaining folds with an embargo; random cross-validation must never\n     be the primary estimate.\n  2. Fit every encoder, scaler, imputer and reducer inside the training fold.\n     The redundancy groups above say what COULD be reduced, not a fitted reducer.\n  3. Report PR-AUC and recall at a fixed FPR per block, and separately for\n     warm-start and cold-start entities; ROC-AUC alone is inadequate at ~3.5%\n     prevalence.\n  4. Treat 'tag_not_drop' features as carried-with-a-tag, and test their removal\n     as an ablation rather than assuming it.\"\"\")","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 13. Thesis Defence Notes\n\nEach note pairs a likely examiner criticism with the evidence this notebook now\nproduces, the limitation that remains, and what it implies for graph-tabular\nfraud modelling.\n\n**13.1 \"Your split leaks — you computed statistics before splitting.\"**\n*Evidence now produced:* the split is derived before any statistic; every fitted\nobject registers its rows and blocks in `LeakageAuditor` and is exported in\n`run_manifest.json`; `guard()` raises at the call site if a quarantined\nidentifier reaches a fit, selection, threshold or clustering call; the quarantine\nassertions run as pass/fail invariants.\n*Remaining limitation:* the guard proves that OOT rows did not enter a *tracked*\ncall. It cannot detect leakage through analyst knowledge — a threshold chosen\nbecause an earlier run's OOT result was disappointing. Only the fixed-before-\nresults discipline and the versioned config snapshot address that.\n*Implication:* a graph model must extend the same registry to edge construction,\ndegrees and community assignments, all of which leak topologically even when\nlabels are hidden.\n\n**13.2 \"Your drift threshold is arbitrary.\"**\n*Evidence now produced:* Spearman replaces an unsourced Pearson rule; Section 10\nshows the full distribution of `|ρ|` and derives a quantile-based alternative;\nPSI and lift stability provide two independent views; every band is configurable\nand snapshotted into each decision.\n*Remaining limitation:* the band boundaries (0.10 / 0.25) remain conventions.\nThey are labelled as configured decision rules everywhere they appear, but they\nare not derived from a loss function.\n*Implication:* the defensible version of \"this feature drifts\" is a cost\nstatement — how much performance is lost by keeping it — which requires a model\nand therefore belongs to the next stage.\n\n**13.3 \"A significant KS test does not mean the drift matters.\"**\n*Evidence now produced:* KS reports statistic, both sample sizes, raw and\nBH-adjusted p-values; the interpretation text states that at these sample sizes\nsignificance is near-automatic; verdicts require PSI or lift evidence, never a\np-value alone.\n*Remaining limitation:* no minimum practically-important KS statistic is\nspecified, because none can be justified without downstream cost information.\n*Implication:* drift monitoring in production should trigger on effect size and\non realised performance, not on test significance.\n\n**13.4 \"Missingness looks predictive because of product mix.\"**\n*Evidence now produced:* patterns collapsed before testing (one test per\nunderlying signal); ProductCD stratification with per-stratum sample and positive\ncounts; an explicit sign-reversal flag; a direction-consistency requirement\nacross development blocks; BH correction.\n*Remaining limitation:* ProductCD is one stratifier. Device, issuer and channel\ncould confound in the same way and are not adjusted for; no multivariate\nadjustment is attempted.\n*Implication:* missingness indicators should enter a graph-tabular model as\nnode/edge *attributes* carrying their provenance, not as free-standing\npredictors, so their product dependence stays visible.\n\n**13.5 \"Your V-series groups are an artefact of one sample.\"**\n*Evidence now produced:* per-block clustering, cross-block co-membership,\nretention only above a configured stability threshold, representatives chosen by\ndevelopment mutual information with deterministic tie-breaking and a recorded\nreason; the sampling approximation is labelled per block.\n*Remaining limitation:* the distance threshold and the stability threshold are\nboth conventions, and correlation-based redundancy ignores interactions — two\nweakly correlated columns can still be jointly redundant.\n*Implication:* redundancy findings are inputs to an ablation, not a licence to\ndelete columns before a baseline exists.\n\n**13.6 \"You are treating shared identifiers as identities.\"**\n*Evidence now produced:* no synthetic customer key is constructed; entity\nstatistics are reported per column with explicit denominators; the text states\nthat anonymised card/address/email/device values are shared attributes, not\nverified persons; degree analysis is bipartite transaction-to-value.\n*Remaining limitation:* without ground-truth linkage, entity resolution quality\nis unknown, and both false merges and false splits are possible in any downstream\ngraph.\n*Implication:* every graph result must be reported against a stated linkage rule,\nand a shuffled-edge ablation is required before any relational lift is believed.\n\n**13.7 \"The 5-block split drives your conclusions.\"**\n*Evidence now produced:* the full diagnostic recomputation at 4, 5, 6 and 8\nblocks with per-configuration re-learning, verdict-change tracking and an\nexported summary; features whose verdicts move are demoted and named.\n*Remaining limitation:* all configurations use equal-row-count blocks on one\ndataset over one ~6-month window; equal-duration blocks and other datasets are\nuntested.\n*Implication:* forward-validation window design should be treated as a reported\nsensitivity, not a fixed hyperparameter.\n\n**13.8 \"You report no model performance.\"**\n*Evidence now produced:* the evaluation function is defined, documented,\ndenominator-explicit and unit-tested on synthetic data; no metric is invented for\na model that does not exist.\n*Remaining limitation:* the audit's practical importance is therefore unquantified\n— it constrains modelling without demonstrating a performance benefit.\n*Implication:* the first modelling deliverable should be a tabular baseline under\nthese constraints, with the graph ladder attempted only after entity-history\nfeatures demonstrate lift.\n\n**13.9 \"Generalizability.\"**\nThis is one competition dataset, anonymised, covering a single ~6-month relative\nwindow from one payment population. Nothing here establishes that the same\nfeatures drift, the same fields are informative when blank, or the same\ncold-start rates hold anywhere else. The transferable content is the protocol and\nthe tooling, not the feature verdicts.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 14. Function Validation — unit-style checks on synthetic data\n\nThese verify the helper functions' documented behaviour, including that the\nguards **fail** when they should. Synthetic data only: nothing here is evidence\nabout IEEE-CIS.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 15. Run Manifest and Artifact Export\n\n`export_run_artifacts` writes `run_manifest.json` and verifies every declared\nartifact exists on disk — a silent write failure cannot be reported as success.\nThe manifest carries the configuration, the environment, the data hash, the full\nfitted-object registry with the blocks each object was fitted on, the leakage\nchecks, the synthetic checks and a byte-level hash of every file written.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\"\"\"Section 14 - unit-style checks on small synthetic data.\n\nThese run on synthetic frames only. They are not evidence about IEEE-CIS; they\nverify that the helper functions behave as their docstrings claim, including the\nguards that are supposed to fail.\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _synthetic_frame(n: int = 600, seed: int = 0) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    rng = np.random.RandomState(seed)\n    return pd.DataFrame({\n        ID_COLUMN: np.arange(1, n + 1),\n        TIME_COLUMN: np.arange(1, n + 1) * 100,\n        \"elapsed_days\": np.arange(1, n + 1) / 10.0,\n        TARGET_COLUMN: rng.binomial(1, 0.1, n),\n        \"feat\": rng.normal(size=n),\n        \"entity\": rng.choice([f\"e{i}\" for i in range(20)], size=n),\n    })\n\n\nTEST_RESULTS: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []\n\n\ndef check(name: str, condition: bool, detail: str = \"\") -> None:\n    TEST_RESULTS.append({\"check\": name, \"passed\": bool(condition), \"detail\": detail})\n    if not condition:\n        raise AssertionError(f\"Synthetic check failed: {name}. {detail}\")\n\n\nrng = np.random.RandomState(RANDOM_SEED)\n\n# 1. PSI is ~0 for identical distributions and rises for a shifted one.\n_base = rng.normal(size=20_000)\n_same = calculate_psi(_base, _base.copy())[\"psi\"]\n_shifted = calculate_psi(_base, _base + 1.0)[\"psi\"]\ncheck(\"psi_zero_for_identical_distributions\", abs(_same) < 1e-9, f\"psi={_same:.3e}\")\ncheck(\"psi_increases_under_a_shift\", _shifted > _same + 0.25,\n      f\"identical={_same:.4f} vs shifted={_shifted:.4f}\")\ncheck(\"psi_bins_learned_from_reference_only\",\n      calculate_psi(_base, _base + 1.0, edges=learn_psi_bins(_base))[\"psi\"] == _shifted,\n      \"supplying reference-learned edges reproduces the same PSI\")\n\n# 2. A moderate and a severe shift land in the configured bands in order.\n_small = calculate_psi(_base, _base + 0.15)[\"psi\"]\ncheck(\"psi_is_monotone_in_shift_size\", _small < _shifted, f\"{_small:.4f} < {_shifted:.4f}\")\n\n# 3. Missing values form their own bin.\n_with_missing = pd.Series(np.concatenate([_base[:10_000], np.full(10_000, np.nan)]))\ncheck(\"psi_missing_bin_registers_drift\",\n      calculate_psi(pd.Series(_base), _with_missing)[\"psi\"] > 0.25,\n      \"a feature that becomes 50% missing must not read as stable\")\n\n# 4. Temporal blocks are ordered, exhaustive and mutually exclusive.\n_frame = _synthetic_frame()\n_frame[\"chrono_block\"] = create_temporal_blocks(_frame, 5)\n_bounds = _frame.groupby(\"chrono_block\")[TIME_COLUMN].agg([\"min\", \"max\"]).sort_index()\ncheck(\"blocks_are_ordered_and_disjoint\",\n      bool((_bounds[\"max\"].values[:-1] < _bounds[\"min\"].values[1:]).all())\n      and _frame[\"chrono_block\"].nunique() == 5,\n      \"each synthetic block ends before the next begins\")\n\n# 5. OOT overlap must TRIGGER the assertion, not pass quietly.\n_dev = _frame[_frame.chrono_block < 5]\n_oot = _frame[_frame.chrono_block == 5]\n_auditor = LeakageAuditor(_oot[ID_COLUMN], [5])\nassert_oot_quarantine(_dev, _oot, _auditor, predictors=[\"feat\"])\n_overlap_detected = False\ntry:\n    assert_oot_quarantine(_frame, _oot, LeakageAuditor(_oot[ID_COLUMN], [5]))\nexcept AssertionError:\n    _overlap_detected = True\ncheck(\"oot_overlap_triggers_an_assertion\", _overlap_detected,\n      \"passing the full frame as 'development' must fail the quarantine check\")\n\n_guard_detected = False\ntry:\n    _auditor.guard(\"deliberate_violation\", _frame)\nexcept AssertionError:\n    _guard_detected = True\ncheck(\"auditor_guard_rejects_oot_rows\", _guard_detected,\n      \"guard() must refuse a frame containing quarantined identifiers\")\n\n_register_detected = False\ntry:\n    _auditor.register_fit(\"deliberate_violation\", \"scaler\", _frame[ID_COLUMN].to_numpy(), [1, 5])\nexcept AssertionError:\n    _register_detected = True\ncheck(\"registering_a_fit_on_oot_is_rejected\", _register_detected,\n      \"register_fit() must refuse a declared fit on the OOT block\")\n\n# 6. Cold-start rate matches a hand-computed expectation on both denominators.\n_cold_dev = pd.DataFrame({ID_COLUMN: range(1, 5), \"entity\": [\"a\", \"a\", \"b\", \"b\"],\n                          TARGET_COLUMN: [0, 1, 0, 0]})\n_cold_oot = pd.DataFrame({ID_COLUMN: range(5, 15),\n                          \"entity\": [\"a\", \"b\", \"z\", \"z\", \"y\", None, None, \"a\", \"x\", \"b\"],\n                          TARGET_COLUMN: [0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0]})\n_cold = calculate_cold_start_rate(_cold_dev, _cold_oot, \"entity\")\n# 8 OOT rows carry a value; 4 of them (z, z, y, x) are unseen in dev.\ncheck(\"cold_start_rate_primary_denominator\", abs(_cold[\"cold_start_rate\"] - 4 / 8) < 1e-12,\n      f\"expected 0.5, got {_cold['cold_start_rate']}\")\ncheck(\"cold_start_rate_all_rows_denominator\",\n      abs(_cold[\"cold_start_rate_all_oot_rows\"] - 4 / 10) < 1e-12,\n      f\"expected 0.4, got {_cold['cold_start_rate_all_oot_rows']}\")\ncheck(\"cold_start_counts_are_consistent\",\n      _cold[\"cold_start_rows\"] + _cold[\"warm_start_rows\"] == _cold[\"oot_rows_non_missing\"],\n      \"warm + cold must exhaust the non-missing OOT rows\")\n\n# 7. Representative selection uses development target data only.\n#\n# The clustering function requires MIN_NON_MISSING observations for each\n# feature in each block. Build enough rows so every development block meets\n# that configured requirement.\n_mi_rows = 5 * max(CONFIG[\"MIN_NON_MISSING\"], 1_200)\n_mi_frame = _synthetic_frame(n=_mi_rows, seed=3)\n_mi_frame[\"chrono_block\"] = create_temporal_blocks(_mi_frame, 5)\n_mi_frame[\"a\"] = _mi_frame[\"feat\"]\n_mi_frame[\"b\"] = _mi_frame[\"feat\"] * 2 + 1e-6\n_mi_dev = _mi_frame.loc[_mi_frame[\"chrono_block\"] < 5].copy()\n_mi_oot = _mi_frame.loc[_mi_frame[\"chrono_block\"] == 5].copy()\n_maps = {\n    block: cluster_v_features_by_block(\n        _mi_dev,\n        [\"a\", \"b\"],\n        block=block,\n    )[\"cluster_map\"]\n    for block in [1, 2, 3, 4]\n}\ncheck(\n    \"representative_test_has_assessable_cluster_maps\",\n    all({\"a\", \"b\"}.issubset(cluster_map) for cluster_map in _maps.values()),\n    (\n        \"each development block must contain enough observations for both \"\n        f\"features; configured minimum is {CONFIG['MIN_NON_MISSING']:,}\"\n    ),\n)\n_stab = assess_cluster_stability(_maps, [\"a\", \"b\"])\n_reps = select_cluster_representatives(\n    _mi_dev,\n    _stab,\n)\ncheck(\n    \"perfectly_correlated_features_co_cluster\",\n    all(\n        cluster_map[\"a\"] == cluster_map[\"b\"]\n        for cluster_map in _maps.values()\n    ),\n    \"distance 1 - |Spearman| should be approximately zero for a monotone transform\",\n)\ncheck(\n    \"representative_selection_returns_one_representative\",\n    (\n        not _reps.empty\n        and \"is_representative\" in _reps.columns\n        and int(_reps[\"is_representative\"].sum()) == 1\n    ),\n    \"the stable two-feature cluster should produce exactly one representative\",\n)\n_representatives_before = (\n    _reps.loc[_reps[\"is_representative\"], \"feature\"]\n    .sort_values()\n    .tolist()\n)\n\n# Poison only the OOT labels. The development frame passed to representative\n# selection remains unchanged.\n_mi_oot[TARGET_COLUMN] = 1 - _mi_oot[TARGET_COLUMN]\n_reps_poisoned = select_cluster_representatives(\n    _mi_dev,\n    _stab,\n)\n_representatives_after = (\n    _reps_poisoned.loc[\n        _reps_poisoned[\"is_representative\"],\n        \"feature\",\n    ]\n    .sort_values()\n    .tolist()\n)\ncheck(\n    \"representative_selection_ignores_oot_target\",\n    _representatives_before == _representatives_after,\n    \"inverting every OOT label must not change a development-only selection\",\n)\n\n# 8. Lift, lift stability and the fixed-FPR evaluator behave as documented.\n_lift_frame = pd.DataFrame({\"x\": np.arange(1000.0), TARGET_COLUMN: (np.arange(1000) >= 900).astype(int)})\n_lift = calculate_top_decile_lift(_lift_frame, \"x\", cut_point=900.0)\ncheck(\"top_decile_lift_detects_a_perfect_signal\", _lift[\"lift\"] > 9,\n      f\"lift={_lift['lift']:.2f} against a 10% base rate\")\ncheck(\"lift_reports_its_denominators\",\n      _lift[\"n_flagged\"] == 100 and _lift[\"positives_flagged\"] == 100,\n      \"flagged count and positive count must both be returned\")\ncheck(\"lift_stability_is_one_for_identical_lifts\",\n      calculate_lift_stability({1: 2.0, 2: 2.0, 3: 2.0})[\"lift_stability\"] == 1.0)\ncheck(\"lift_stability_is_withheld_without_enough_blocks\",\n      calculate_lift_stability({1: 2.0})[\"verdict\"] == \"insufficient_evidence\")\n_eval = recall_at_fixed_fpr([0] * 990 + [1] * 10, list(np.arange(990.0)) + [2000.0] * 10, fpr=0.01)\ncheck(\"recall_at_fixed_fpr_is_exact_on_a_separable_case\", _eval[\"recall\"] == 1.0,\n      f\"realised FPR {_eval['realised_fpr']:.4f} on {_eval['n_negatives']} negatives\")\n\n# 9. Multiplicity correction is monotone and never below the raw p-value.\n_p = np.array([0.001, 0.01, 0.04, 0.2, np.nan])\n_adj = adjust_multiple_tests(_p)\ncheck(\"bh_adjusted_pvalues_are_not_smaller_than_raw\",\n      bool((_adj.adjusted_pvalue.dropna().to_numpy()\n            >= _p[np.isfinite(_p)] - 1e-12).all()))\ncheck(\"bh_carries_nan_through\", bool(np.isnan(_adj.adjusted_pvalue.iloc[-1])),\n      \"an unrunnable test must not be counted in the multiplicity correction\")\n\n# 10. Empty, constant and all-missing inputs degrade to \"not assessable\".\ncheck(\"constant_feature_is_not_assessable\",\n      not calculate_psi(np.ones(100), np.ones(100))[\"assessable\"])\ncheck(\"all_missing_feature_is_not_assessable\",\n      not calculate_psi([np.nan] * 100, [np.nan] * 100)[\"assessable\"])\ncheck(\"empty_lift_input_is_not_assessable\",\n      not calculate_top_decile_lift(\n          pd.DataFrame({\"x\": [np.nan] * 5, TARGET_COLUMN: [0] * 5}), \"x\", 1.0)[\"assessable\"])\n\n# 11. Deterministic sampling respects the configured row cap.\n_sample_source = pd.DataFrame({\"x\": np.arange(119_999)})\n_sampled = evenly_spaced_sample(_sample_source, 60_000)\ncheck(\"evenly_spaced_sampling_respects_row_cap\", len(_sampled) == 60_000,\n      f\"expected 60,000 rows, got {len(_sampled):,}\")\ncheck(\"evenly_spaced_sampling_keeps_endpoints\",\n      int(_sampled.iloc[0, 0]) == 0 and int(_sampled.iloc[-1, 0]) == 119_998,\n      \"deterministic sample should cover the full ordered block\")\n\n# 12. Hashing is deterministic and order-independent under the declared sort.\n_h1 = compute_dataframe_hash(_frame, columns=[ID_COLUMN, TIME_COLUMN], sort_by=[TIME_COLUMN, ID_COLUMN])\n_h2 = compute_dataframe_hash(_frame.sample(frac=1, random_state=7),\n                             columns=[ID_COLUMN, TIME_COLUMN], sort_by=[TIME_COLUMN, ID_COLUMN])\ncheck(\"dataframe_hash_is_order_independent_after_sorting\", _h1[\"sha256\"] == _h2[\"sha256\"])\n\n# 13. The ledger is JSON-serialisable with nulls preserved and contracts enforced.\n_ledger = update_decisions_ledger({}, \"synthetic_feature\", \"other\",\n                                  {\"psi_max\": 0.02, \"ks_stat\": None},\n                                  \"stable\", \"synthetic check\", \"keep\",\n                                  [\"14. Function Validation\"], {\"RANDOM_SEED\": RANDOM_SEED},\n                                  \"deadbeef\", CONFIG[\"CODE_VERSION\"])\n_round_trip = json.loads(json.dumps(_ledger, allow_nan=False))\ncheck(\"ledger_is_json_serialisable\", _round_trip[\"synthetic_feature\"][\"verdict\"] == \"stable\")\ncheck(\"ledger_preserves_null_metrics\",\n      _round_trip[\"synthetic_feature\"][\"diagnostic_results\"][\"ks_stat\"] is None,\n      \"a metric that was not computed must stay null, never 0\")\ncheck(\"ledger_records_decision_scope\",\n      _round_trip[\"synthetic_feature\"][\"decision_scope\"] == \"development_only\")\n_rejected = False\ntry:\n    update_decisions_ledger({}, \"f\", \"other\", {}, \"great\", \"\", \"keep\", [\"x\"], {}, \"h\", \"v\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    _rejected = True\ncheck(\"ledger_rejects_an_unknown_verdict\", _rejected)\n_rejected = False\ntry:\n    update_decisions_ledger({}, \"f\", \"other\", {}, \"stable\", \"\", \"keep\", [\"x\"], {}, \"h\", \"v\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    _rejected = True\ncheck(\"ledger_requires_a_reason_for_every_verdict\", _rejected)\n\n# 14. Schema validation fails loudly on a missing required column.\n_rejected = False\ntry:\n    validate_schema(pd.DataFrame({\"a\": [1]}), [\"a\", \"b\"], name=\"synthetic\")\nexcept KeyError:\n    _rejected = True\ncheck(\"schema_validation_names_missing_required_columns\", _rejected)\n\nTEST_TABLE = pd.DataFrame(TEST_RESULTS)\nprint(TEST_TABLE.to_string(index=False))\nprint(f\"\\n{int(TEST_TABLE.passed.sum())} / {len(TEST_TABLE)} synthetic checks passed.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\"\"\"Section 15 - run manifest and artifact export.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef export_run_artifacts(output_directory: str, config: Mapping[str, Any],\n                         environment: Mapping[str, Any], data_hash: Mapping[str, Any],\n                         auditor: LeakageAuditor, schema_report: Mapping[str, Any],\n                         findings: Mapping[str, Any],\n                         test_table: pd.DataFrame,\n                         artifact_paths: Mapping[str, Optional[str]]) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Write run_manifest.json and verify every declared artifact exists on disk.\n\n    The manifest is the reproducibility record: configuration, environment,\n    data hash, the full fitted-object registry with the blocks each object was\n    fitted on, the leakage-check outcomes, the synthetic-check outcomes and the\n    artifact inventory with a byte-level hash of each file actually written.\n\n    Raises:\n        FileNotFoundError: if an artifact was declared but is not on disk, so a\n            silent write failure cannot be mistaken for a successful run.\n    \"\"\"\n    os.makedirs(output_directory, exist_ok=True)\n    inventory = []\n    for name, path in artifact_paths.items():\n        if path is None:\n            inventory.append({\"artifact\": name, \"path\": None, \"written\": False,\n                              \"reason\": \"not computed in this run\"})\n            continue\n        if not os.path.exists(path):\n            raise FileNotFoundError(\n                f\"Artifact '{name}' was declared at {path} but does not exist. \"\n                \"The run is incomplete; do not report its contents.\")\n        record = hash_file(path)\n        inventory.append({\"artifact\": name, \"path\": path, \"written\": True, **record})\n\n    manifest = {\n        \"run\": {\n            \"code_version\": config[\"CODE_VERSION\"],\n            \"source_notebook\": config[\"SOURCE_NOTEBOOK_REF\"],\n            \"executed_at_utc\": datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec=\"seconds\"),\n        },\n        \"environment\": dict(environment),\n        \"configuration\": {k: v for k, v in config.items()},\n        \"data_hash\": dict(data_hash),\n        \"schema\": {k: v for k, v in schema_report.items()},\n        \"leakage_checks\": auditor.checks,\n        \"fitted_objects\": [record.to_dict() for record in auditor.registry],\n        \"fitted_object_count_by_kind\": (\n            pd.Series([r.kind for r in auditor.registry]).value_counts().to_dict()\n            if auditor.registry else {}),\n        \"synthetic_checks\": test_table.to_dict(orient=\"records\") if len(test_table) else [],\n        \"findings\": dict(findings),\n        \"artifacts\": inventory,\n    }\n    path = os.path.join(output_directory, \"run_manifest.json\")\n    with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n        json.dump(manifest, handle, indent=2, default=str, allow_nan=False)\n    return {\"manifest_path\": path, \"manifest\": manifest}\n\n\nARTIFACT_PATHS = {\n    \"feature_risk_table\": FEATURE_RISK_PATH,\n    \"decisions_ledger\": LEDGER_PATH,\n    \"entity_cold_start_summary\": ENTITY_COLD_START_PATH,\n    \"block_sensitivity_summary\": BLOCK_SENSITIVITY_PATH,\n}\n\nEXPORT = export_run_artifacts(OUTPUT_DIRECTORY, CONFIG, RUN_ENVIRONMENT, DATA_HASH,\n                              AUDITOR, SCHEMA_REPORT, FINDINGS, TEST_TABLE,\n                              ARTIFACT_PATHS)\n\nprint(f\"Artifacts written to {os.path.abspath(OUTPUT_DIRECTORY)}:\")\nprint(pd.DataFrame(EXPORT[\"manifest\"][\"artifacts\"]).to_string(index=False))\nprint(f\"\\nrun_manifest.json: {EXPORT['manifest_path']}\")\nprint(f\"Fitted objects registered: {len(AUDITOR.registry)} \"\n      f\"({EXPORT['manifest']['fitted_object_count_by_kind']}) - every one recorded \"\n      f\"with the development blocks it was fitted on, none touching block {OOT_BLOCK}.\")\nprint(f\"Leakage checks: {int(CHECKS.passed.sum())} passed, \"\n      f\"{int((~CHECKS.passed).sum())} failed.\")\nprint(f\"Data hash (SHA-256): {DATA_HASH['sha256']}\")\nprint(\"\\nNo credential, token, username or absolute local path is written into any \"\n      \"artifact: only basenames of input files, their content hashes and the \"\n      \"configuration dictionary above are recorded.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Reproduction instructions\n\n1. Attach the **IEEE-CIS Fraud Detection** competition data, or set\n   `CANDIDATE_DIRS` to a directory containing `train_transaction.csv` and\n   `train_identity.csv`.\n2. Ensure `numpy`, `pandas`, `scipy`, `scikit-learn` and `matplotlib` are\n   installed. The dependency cell fails with the full list if any is absent.\n3. **Restart the kernel and run all cells in order.** The notebook holds no\n   hidden state and no cell depends on being run out of order; a restart-and-\n   run-all is the intended execution mode and the way hidden state is detected.\n4. Artifacts appear in `CONFIG[\"OUTPUT_DIRECTORY\"]` (`./artifacts` by default):\n   `feature_risk_table.csv`, `decisions_ledger.json`,\n   `entity_cold_start_summary.csv`, `block_sensitivity_summary.csv`,\n   `run_manifest.json`.\n5. Verify the run by checking that `run_manifest.json` reports zero failed\n   leakage checks, that every synthetic check passed, and that the data hash\n   matches the one you expect for your copy of the data.\n\n**Execution status of this file as delivered:** the notebook is complete and\nrunnable, but has **not** been executed against the IEEE-CIS data in the\nenvironment where it was authored (the dataset was not present there). All output\nareas are therefore empty by design — no cached, illustrative or reconstructed\nresult appears anywhere in this file. Every number in this notebook will be\nproduced by your run, and until then all findings are **pending**, not results.","metadata":{}}]}